# Changelog

All notable changes to `urna` are documented here.

Format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/). Versioning follows [SemVer](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html). The on-disk container format is frozen at v1; breaking changes bump `URNA_FORMAT_VERSION` (binary) or `URNA_SCHEMA_VERSION` (manifest fields).

## [Unreleased]

### Added

- `.github/zizmor.yml` records the 39 zizmor findings kept on purpose, ignored per audit and per file, each with its reason (#517, part of #519), so zizmor reports none. 29 are cargo-dist's template in `release.yml` and `rehearsal.yml`, which no dist setting changes and a hand edit would lose: template injection in dist's own steps, workflow-level `contents: write`, `secrets: inherit`, a `container:` that is null for all five targets, `./` reusable-workflow calls and token publishing. 10 are choices of the hand-written workflows: `wheelmake.yml` takes its caller's permissions, `runreport.yml` learns of a finished release through `workflow_run`, `setuptest.yml` installs from each channel as its test, `rustready.yml` publishes with a token, and `wheelmake.yml` and `pypiindex.yml` call `./`, since actionlint 1.7.12 refuses GitHub's newer `$/` (rhysd/actionlint#711). `tool/tasks/namecheck.py` exempts the file at that path: zizmor finds its config by that name.
- `.github/workflows/lintworks.yml` checks the workflows on every change under `.github/workflows`, without running a job. actionlint (1.7.12, the release binary checked against its sha256) gates the pull request: syntax, `${{ }}` expressions, `needs`, runner labels and, through shellcheck at warning and above, every `run:` script. It ignores one finding by name: `release.yml` and `rehearsal.yml` read `steps.cargo-cyclonedx.output.paths` where the step has `outputs`, so the SBOM path comes out empty; the line is cargo-dist's template and neither file is hand-edited, so the fix belongs upstream. zizmor (1.30.1, through `zizmor-action`) audits the workflows for security and uploads its findings to code scanning instead of failing the job, because the workflows carry 156 findings from before the check; new ones show on the pull request that adds them. A pull request from a fork gets a read-only token, so zizmor skips it and audits the change on the push to `main` after the merge. Both run locally too: `actionlint` and `zizmor .` from the root.
- `docs/TERMS.md`, the lexicon of the abbreviations in the repository's names (one meaning each, entered in the pull request that creates the name), and `tool/tasks/namecheck.py` with `tool/tests/test_namecheck.py`, which check the length table of ADR-0003, its exceptions and the lexicon on the tracked files in `fullcheck.sh` and `gatecheck.yml` (#479).
- Refactoring tools for Python moves, configured in `pyproject.toml`: `[tool.pyright]` checks only that imports resolve, with the dev `sys.path` (`python/`, `python/tools/`, `script/`) as extra paths; `[tool.rope]` gives Rope the same source folders so a module move rewrites the imports in `tests/` and `python/tools/`; `[tool.importlinter]` and the `lint` dependency group add a contract that `forge` never imports `python/tools`. CONTRIBUTING has the commands.
- `tool/tests/test_atlasdocs.py` (#509; 4 cases, in fullcheck, CI and the ruff list) holds `docs/ATLAS.toml`'s `summary` to the current state: at most three sentences (`e.g.` and `i.e.` do not end one), 639 characters, and no history (dates, tracker ids such as `p1#09` or `#262`, release versions such as `v0.6`, change verbs such as `added` or `moved`).

### Changed

- Every action in the workflows runs at a fixed commit instead of a moving tag (#515, part of #519): zizmor's 66 `unpinned-uses` findings, more than half of code scanning's 123. The hand-written workflows name it as `@<sha> # vX.Y.Z`; `release.yml` takes the same commits from `[workspace.metadata.dist.github-action-commits]` in `Cargo.toml`, and `tool/tasks/rehearsal.py` reads that table for the jobs it adds to `rehearsal.yml`, so one table moves an action for both. Same versions as before (checkout 6.1.0, setup-python 7.0.0, upload-artifact 7.0.2, download-artifact 8.0.2, cache 6.1.0, attest 4.2.2, setup-node 5.0.0 and 6.5.0). Every checkout in `gatecheck.yml`, `setuptest.yml` and `tagverify.yml` also sets `persist-credentials: false` (zizmor's 13 `artipacked` findings, on the lines the pins touch), so the token does not stay in `.git/config`; none of these jobs pushes or fetches after it. The npm publish job's `setup-node` sets `package-manager-cache: false` (zizmor's `cache-poisoning`): a job that publishes restores no cache, and setup-node v5+ caches by default. dist has no setting for it, so `tool/tasks/distpatch.py generate` runs dist and applies the edit, matching dist's text exactly once; `allow-dirty = ["ci"]` keeps dist's plan from refusing the edited file, and `gatecheck.yml` regenerates `release.yml` with the pinned dist (0.32.0, sha256 checked) and fails one that differs, so the file stays dist's output plus that edit.
- The hand-written workflows keep `${{ }}` out of their shell scripts (#516, part of #519): zizmor's 4 `template-injection` findings outside the generated files. `tagverify.yml` reads the tag input through `env:` and the ref type from `$GITHUB_REF_TYPE`; the fuzz soak in `gatecheck.yml` takes its target and seconds as `$TARGET` and `$SOAK_SECONDS`; `pypiindex.yml` passes the version as `$VERSION`.
- `docs/ATLAS.toml` keeps the current state only, and its header says so (#509, part of #511). The inventory entries of `presetrun.py` and `benchtime.py` describe where the variants build and which baseline fullcheck gates against, instead of `data/measure/` and the LFS baseline. The `summary` had grown into a changelog of dated paragraphs (17,789 characters); it is three sentences on the format, the engine, the CLI, the Python bridge and the ingest workspace. The history was already in this file except three items, now added: the p1#08 flagship (0.3.0), task 1 of #262 and the pt-BR corpus build removal (0.5.2).
- The workspace lints add `unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn`, `missing_debug_implementations` and `rust_2018_idioms` (rustc) and `dbg_macro`, `todo` and `expect_used` (clippy), all `deny` (#510, part of #511). The paths that hid a lifetime name it (`Cursor<'_>`). Every public type is `Debug`: the thirteen that hold file bytes, the mmap or corpus-sized vectors (`MmapUrnaFile`, `HnswIndex`, `Bm25Index`, `CsrIndex`, `Traversal`, `UrnaView`, `UrnaFileBuilder`, `CsrParts`, `Deduped`, the int8/int4 views, `TxtStreams`, `IntpackReader`) print their shape through a crate-private `shape_debug!`, which names every field (`show`, `len`, `some` or `skip`) and destructures them all, so a field added later does not compile until it is named; the rest derive it. The two benches and one proptest helper allow `expect`. `rust/format` forbids `unsafe_code` (the engine's SIMD keeps the workspace from doing so). `rust/ingest` and `fuzz/`, workspaces of their own, carry a copy of the tables (`rust/ingest` also `unsafe_code = "forbid"` and `rust-version = "1.85"`); `tool/tests/test_lintmatch.py` (4 cases, in fullcheck, CI and the ruff list) holds both copies to the root's, and CI runs Clippy on `fuzz/` on stable. CONTRIBUTING, SECURITY and the atlas list the eight lints.
- No tool reads `data/` any more (#508, part of #511; #503 removed it). `presetrun.py` measures the pinned benchmark corpus (`tool/tasks/benchdata.py`) when no `--baseline` is given and builds its variants under `target/bench/<first 16 hex of the baseline file_hash>/`, so `--reuse` never measures another corpus's builds; `test_askrouter.py` reads the pinned corpus's there. Before any step, `fullcheck.sh` checks the baseline against the corpus (`benchdata.py match`): exit 9 when `URNA_BASELINE` names no usable file, exit 10 when its `baseline_file_hash` is not the corpus's sha-256, so a run on another corpus is never gated against the pinned corpus's numbers; exit 3 keeps meaning the corpus is not cached. `benchgate.py` refuses a baseline and a post of two corpora too. The usage examples of the image tools take a placeholder path.
- `gatecheck.yml`'s 639-line guard lists the files with `find -exec wc -l {} +` instead of `find | xargs wc -l`, which shellcheck flags (SC2038) for file names with spaces. Same result on the current tree.
- `docs/ARCHS.toml` is `docs/ATLAS.toml`. The architecture map needed a five-letter name for the docs folder, and `ARCHS` was an abbreviation made up to fill the length; `ATLAS` is a whole word for what the file is, so the `archs` entry leaves `docs/TERMS.md`. The README, CONTRIBUTING, the ADR index, the format crate's comments and the file's own inventory point to the new path. No format or runtime change.
- The repository moved to seven four-letter root folders and one Python package (#479, part of #296; ADR-0003 supersedes ADR-0002). `crates/` is `rust/`, `script/` is `tool/tasks/`, `tests/` is `tool/tests/`, the benchmarks of `python/tools/` are `tool/bench/`, `packs/` is `pkgs/wheel/` (the wheel stages into `pkgs/stage/`), `demos/` is `demo/` (`quickstart` is `starter`, `flask` is `flaskpy`), `assets/` is `docs/img/`, `docs/ADR` is `docs/adr` with lowercase categories, `docs/ARC.toml` is `docs/ARCHS.toml`. `python/` and `python/forge/` dissolve into the `urna` package at `rust/bridge/python/urna/`, with the subpackages `embed`, `entry`, `gates`, `image`, `model`, `pipes`, `reads` and `specs` and nine-letter modules; the forge's self-tests join `tool/tests/`, the demo corpus is `demo/corpora/intro/`, and the potion table is `urna/model/potionb8m/` (the model id stays `minishlab/potion-base-8M`). What users see in the next release: the embedder payload installs under `<data root>/urna/python/urna/`, so an existing install needs `urna setup` again, which lays the new payload down and removes the previous one (`forge/`, `embed_query.py`, `model_fingerprint.py` and their `__pycache__/` in the data dir); `urna setup --uninstall` and the one-liner installers remove them too. The one-liner installer moves to `tool/tasks/installer.sh` (and `.ps1`) and `script/` is gone, so the `script/installer.sh` URL the 0.5.4 pages print answers 404 until the next release replaces them. The wheel follows the package: `urna.embed_potion` is `urna.embed.potiontab`, its table is `urna/model/potionb8m/`, and the console script runs `urna.entry.clidriver`. `import urna` no longer loads the `_urna` extension: it loads on the first `urna.open`, `urna.UrnaFile` or `urna.build`, so the query embedders import the package in the payload, which carries no extension. No format or runtime change.
- Every pull request is tied to an issue, written down in CONTRIBUTING and the pull request template: one coherent change is one issue and one PR; several independent items are an epic with one GitHub sub-issue each, each with its PR. Issues and PRs get the right label (created when missing), and the milestone and project views once the repository has them. A PR starts with `Closes #<sub-issue>. Part of #<epic>.`, opens from the updated `main`, and lists its checks as checkboxes with results and the tested commit. When a series closes, the epic body becomes its history.
- Pull requests merge with a merge commit, the one method `main` allows now, so every commit of a pull request stays in its history. Only the maintainer merges: an agent opens the pull request, asks for a review and stops there, and asks before pushing a release tag. CONTRIBUTING says so; they had said one-commit pull requests were squash merged and the rest rebase merged. The `opensource` ruleset no longer requires a linear history, and squash and rebase merges are off.
- `main`'s history has the commits of ten merged pull requests back (#404, #417 to #420, #422, #448, #449, #453, #454), which squash merges and a GitHub rebase merge had left out. One merge that keeps the tree adds them, the way #405 did (#456).
- The README's image table says which mtg-urna-benchmark file each row is (#450). The forge calls AVIF q48 `stills` and the old AV1 all-intra recipe `stills-av1`; the benchmark's v0.3 `stills` file is that AV1 build (1.37 GB, 2.89x) and AVIF q48 is its candidate `v03-avif-q48`, so a reader who followed the link found another `stills`. The numbers already agreed with the benchmark's RESULTS.md.

### Fixed

- sentence-transformers 6 renamed `get_sentence_embedding_dimension` to `get_embedding_dimension` and warns on the old name on every query (#508). `searchtxt.dimension` calls the new name and falls back to the old one, which the supported floor (5.7) may still need; `searchtxt.py` and the registry's text adapter go through it.
- setuptest's Windows CLI leg validated the golden fixture at `crates\urna-format\...`, a backslash path the folder rename to `crates/format` did not match, so that leg failed on the v0.5.4 release run while the release and every channel were fine. It points at `crates\format\` now; dispatched against v0.5.4 with the fix, setuptest passed all 20 legs (run 37256022536).

### Security

- Third-party actions in `gatecheck.yml`, `wheelmake.yml` and `pypiindex.yml` are pinned to a commit SHA, with the version in a comment, so a moved tag upstream cannot change what runs, the PyPI publish action included. Repository settings to match: a `release-tags` ruleset refuses creating, moving or deleting a `v*` tag to everyone but admins, who bypass it, and the `pypi` environment deploys only from `v*` tags, `testpypi` only from `main`. `main` is guarded by the `opensource` ruleset alone: the classic branch protection that duplicated it is gone, and its two rules of its own (an up-to-date branch, resolved conversations) moved into the ruleset.

## [0.5.4] - 2026-10-04

The release path in one run, and the repository's new names. Every channel publishes from the tag's own run: the four wheels are built, attested and attached to the GitHub release, and PyPI takes those same files through OIDC trusted publishing with PEP 740 provenance, the first release whose PyPI upload used no token. crates.io gets `urna-engine`, the renamed `urna-runtime`; `cargo install urna`, the archive names, `brew install urna`, `npm i -g @urna/cli` and `pip install urna` are unchanged. No format, runtime or API change (`cargo-semver-checks` passes on `urna-format`).

### Added

- `script/releasepr.sh X.Y.Z` opens the release pull request. It works in a new worktree of `origin/main` on `release-X.Y.Z`, so the current checkout and its uncommitted files never enter it. cargo-release 1.1.6, pinned in the script, sets the workspace version, the `urna-format` and `urna-engine` pins and the lockfile, then moves `[Unreleased]` under a dated section and sets the five versioned fields of `CITATION.cff` (the rest of the file stays). The preflight runs on the result, a change outside those four files is refused, and the commit is signed; only that branch is pushed. The tag stays a separate step after the merge. `tests/test_releasepr.py` (4 cases) runs the real script with the real tool in a scratch repository and a throwaway signing key.
- The release rehearsal: `.github/workflows/rehearsal.yml` runs the release's own build before a tag and publishes nothing. `script/rehearsal.py` generates it from the `release.yml` dist writes, so it is the same plan, the same five archive builds on the same runners and matrix, the same wheels and the same global job (ubuntu-22.04, the system `python3` that stopped the 0.5.2 tag). The transformation is small and strict: it drops the host and publish jobs, sets every permission to read, strips the attestation steps, refuses a kept job that reads a secret, and refuses any job, condition, plan command or top-level key it does not know, so a dist upgrade stops at CI's `generate --check` until it is taught the new shape. An `impact` job runs the builds only when a release input changed (crates, manifests, `packs/`, the staging scripts, every payload and wheel source as the stage scripts list them, the model dirs, `README.md`, `LICENSE`, the release workflows). The `assets` job checks every artifact the plan names, the four wheels and their `.sha256`, every checksum, `sha256.sum` (each line, and an entry for every archive and packaged installer, so an empty or partial index fails), the payload's `VERSION`, and the Linux binary: `--version` must exit 0 and say exactly `urna <version>`, and `validate` must pass on the golden fixture. The `rehearsal` job is the check to require: only an explicit `run=false` from the impact job dispenses it, and a needed build that failed, was cancelled or was skipped fails it. The generator refuses any read of the secrets context other than the run's own token, in dotted, bracketed or whole-context form. It does not rebuild the measurement corpora. `wheelmake.yml` no longer declares permissions and takes its caller's (the release grants the attestation, the rehearsal only reads); its attestation step runs on the tag push only. CI gains `payload-py310`, which stages the payload under Python 3.10. `tests/test_rehearsal.py` (7 cases, the fifteenth gate suite).
- Release channels: `script/chanprobe.py` probes what npm (the version document), crates.io (the sparse index, unyanked), PyPI (the version JSON, with files), the Homebrew formula and the GitHub release serve for one exact version (`0.5.30` is not `0.5.3`). setuptest's registry legs wait for it before installing (bounded at 15 minutes), so a version still propagating is waited for instead of failing the leg as npm did with `ETARGET` on 0.5.3; the wheel leg's fixed retry loop is gone. Every leg checks that `urna --version` is exactly the tag's version, Homebrew included, and the Windows leg checks `--version`, `validate` and `doctor` one by one (pwsh reported only the last command's exit code). rustready tells a published crate (skip) from an absent one (publish) and stops on a 429, a 5xx or no answer after retries, where any curl failure used to read as absent. `.github/workflows/runreport.yml` writes one summary per release when the release run completes, whatever its conclusion: the tag's commit, every job that did not succeed, the `pypiindex.yml` run of that commit, each channel's state, the release's wheels against PyPI's and an attestation per archive and wheel, named by the triggering run's id and commit, not `main`'s. An API that does not answer, answers something unreadable or lists no run is reported as unavailable or absent, never as success, and the summary is written before the job fails. Against the real releases it reports v0.5.3 served everywhere (with no wheels on its GitHub release, which predates them) and v0.5.2 as it happened: the global job failed, six jobs skipped, PyPI served, the GitHub release, crates.io, npm and Homebrew absent. setuptest dispatched against v0.5.3 on the branch passed all 20 jobs. `tests/test_chanprobe.py` (8 cases) runs it against a local HTTP server.
- The wheels build inside the release run (`wheelmake.yml`, a dist local-artifacts job), next to the archives: the host and every publish job wait for all four, and the GitHub release carries them with a `.sha256` and a Sigstore attestation each. Before, `pypiindex.yml` built and published on its own run of the same tag, which is how 0.5.0 and 0.5.2 reached PyPI while the binary release failed.
- PyPI publishes from the release run: the `pypiready.yml` publish job dispatches `pypiindex.yml` on the tag with its run as the wheels' source and waits for that run (asked with `return_run_details`, the dispatch answers 200 with the run id instead of an empty 204; a deadline cancels it). `pypiindex.yml` stays a top-level workflow because PyPI's trusted publishing matches on `job_workflow_ref` and does not support a reusable workflow as the publisher. It has two paths that never mix: `index=pypi` on a signed `v*` tag, taking the wheels and checksums from the GitHub release the host made and verifying each attestation against the signer workflow (`wheelmake.yml`), the tag's commit and the tag's ref; `index=testpypi` on `main`, from a successful rehearsal run, to prove the publisher before a release. The upload (`script/pypiindex.py`) skips a wheel the index already has with the same sha256 and refuses one with another, checking every remote file before copying any, so a refused plan leaves nothing staged and a rerun after a partial upload finishes it. It publishes with OIDC only; the `PYPI_API_TOKEN` secret is no longer read and goes once a release has published this way. `tests/test_pypiindex.py` (5 cases, the fourteenth gate suite) runs it against a local HTTP server.
- `script/preflight.py`: the version a release names agrees everywhere before anything is built. On every pull request (`gatecheck.yml`) it checks the `urna-format` and `urna-engine` pins, every crate inheriting the workspace version, the four crates in `Cargo.lock`, `CITATION.cff` (`version`, the versioned crates.io and release URLs, and a `date-released` equal to the changelog's date, not today's) and the changelog section. On a release tag, `tagverify.yml` runs it after the signature check on the files of the tag's own commit, read through git rather than from the checkout (a checkout carrying a later fix must not vouch for a stale tag), and adds the tag name, the commit's place on `main` and that the release date is not after the tag. Until now these were hand checks in step 8 of the maintainer checklist. `tests/test_preflight.py` (7 cases, the thirteenth gate suite) drifts one field per case and checks tags in throwaway repos, including a tag on a commit with a stale CITATION while main and the checkout carry the fix.
- `docs/ADR/` holds architecture decision records and lessons that must stay as reference, one folder per category (STRUCTURE, CONTAINER, INTERFACE, EMBEDDERS, MODELSZOO, PACKAGING, RETRIEVAL, SAFEGUARD, WORKFLOWS, DATASTORE, OPTIMIZER, STABILITY, ENCODINGS). `docs/ADR/TEMPLATE.md` gives each record a YAML header (id, kind, status, category, dates, tags, supersede links) so `rg` finds them without opening them, and `docs/ADR/README.md` covers when to write one, the numbering and the supersede rule. CONTRIBUTING and the ARC inventory point to it. Operational lessons stay in the doc that owns the topic.

### Changed

- Folders, workflows, scripts and tests carry short names of the same length (#296, ADR-0002): a list of names that line up is easier to scan. Crate folders are six letters with no prefix: `crates/format`, `crates/engine`, `crates/clitui`, `crates/bridge`, and `crates/ingest` (was `forge-core` at the root; still its own cargo workspace, excluded at the root). Published packages keep the `urna-` prefix, which crates.io needs for lack of namespaces: `urna-format`, `urna-engine` (was `urna-runtime`), and the CLI stays `urna`. The unpublished `urna-python` is `urna-bridge` and `forge-core` is `urna-ingest`. Folders: `scripts/` is `script/`, `examples/` is `demos/`, `packaging/` is `packs/`, `assets/images/` is `assets/image/`. Workflows: `gatecheck.yml`, `tagverify.yml`, `wheelmake.yml`, `pypiindex.yml`, `pypiready.yml`, `rustready.yml`, `setuptest.yml`, `rehearsal.yml`, `runreport.yml`, plus `.github/buildprep.yml` and the tag signers file `.github/trustkeys`. Scripts: `rehearsal.py`, `preflight.py`, `pypiindex.py`, `chanprobe.py`, `releasepr.sh`, `fullcheck.sh`, `ruffcheck.sh`, `embedpack.py`, `wheelprep.py`, `getpotion.sh`, `fuzzsweep.sh`, `installer.sh`/`installer.ps1`, `precommit`; their log prefixes follow, and `fullcheck.sh` writes `/tmp/fullcheck_post.json` by default. Tests: each `tests/test_*.py` is named after what it covers (`test_pythonapi`, `test_ingestion`, `test_imagepipe`, `test_nonetwork` and the rest). What users see in this release: crates.io gets `urna-engine` as a new crate (`urna-runtime` stays at 0.5.3); `cargo install urna`, the archives `urna-<target>`, the binary, `brew install urna`, `npm i -g @urna/cli`, the `urna` wheel and `import urna` are unchanged. The one-liner is `script/installer.sh`; the 0.5.3 pages on crates.io, PyPI and npm print the old `scripts/install.sh` and image paths, which answer 404, and the 0.5.4 pages carry the new ones. PyPI's trusted publisher now names `pypiindex.yml`. No format, runtime or API change. A hook copied from `scripts/pre-commit` keeps working.
- The release docs say what is implemented and what a release has shown. Usage and Security separate v0.5.3 (no wheels on its GitHub release, PyPI on a token without PEP 740) from the current workflows (wheels attested and attached, PyPI through OIDC), which 0.5.4 is the first release to run. The tag check stops the wheels, the host and every publish job, not the archive builds that run beside it. A broken channel is no longer answered by "yank and re-cut": crates.io and PyPI versions are permanent, so it is a per-channel decision, usually the next patch. The Homebrew tap and the three crates are described as published. The README says the Python `retrieve` checks the model only when given `expected_model_hash`. The dist config comments in `Cargo.toml` no longer promise an attestation and an SBOM for every artifact (the five archives are attested; one SBOM covers the CLI package), and the comment in `packs/pyproject.toml` gives the full dev build with `pyo3/extension-module`.
- CONTRIBUTING states what CI covers (ruff, the model catalog and the release suites in its Python job, the payload under Python 3.10, the required rehearsal), adds `pyyaml` to the local setup and runs ruff through `script/ruffcheck.sh`. `fullcheck.sh` ends by pointing at `releasepr.sh` instead of an unsigned tag pushed with `--tags`. The root `pyproject.toml` comment gives the dev build with `--features pyo3/extension-module`, like the wheel project's.
- Usage's release verification names what v0.5.3 carries (checked on the release: the five archives with `LICENSE` and a `.sha256`, the payload's `VERSION`, `sha256.sum`, the Sigstore attestation of an archive bound to `refs/tags/v0.5.3` and `7a417e1e`), and PyPI 0.5.0 to 0.5.3 without PEP 740 provenance. Item 11 of the maintainer checklist gains two lessons of the 0.5.2 and 0.5.3 tags: rehearse the global job in the runner's image before tagging, and rerun setuptest's npm legs once the registry serves the version.
- `ARC.toml` moved from `docs/arc/` to `docs/`, beside the other docs. CONTRIBUTING, the README, the format crate's comments and the ARC inventory point to the new path, and the inventory no longer lists a `docs/arc` directory. Doc-location change only; no format or runtime change.
- `LICENSE` moved from `docs/` to the root, the only place GitHub looks for a license: the repository now shows MIT in its sidebar and API, where `docs/LICENSE` gave none. The crates' `LICENSE` links, the CLI's dist `include`, the wheel staging, the rehearsal's release inputs and the README follow. The `Dockerfile` moved from `docker/` to the root beside its `.dockerignore`, so the image builds with `docker build -t urna .`; Usage and the ARC inventory follow.

### Removed

- `llms.txt` at the repository root. The format is a website convention: no tool reads it from a repository, and https://docs.urna.dev/llms.txt already indexes every page, so the repo copy was a second index kept in sync by hand. The ARC inventory and the rehearsal test no longer name it.

### Fixed

- The Flask, FastAPI and Jupyter examples called `retrieve` without `expected_model_hash` or `query_text`: pointed at a corpus built with another model they answered with cosine-valid, wrong hits, and a corpus with a BM25 section never took the hybrid route. They now pass `expected_model_hash=emb.model_hash()` and the query text, and their READMEs state Python 3.12+ and that the corpus must come from the same embedder. Checked against the wheel the rehearsal built on `main` (`839981b9`): each answers the demo query with citations and refuses a corpus from another model with `model_hash mismatch`.
- A release prepared and tagged the same evening in Brasilia was refused. cargo-release dates the release in UTC, so after 21:00 at -03:00 the changelog carries the next day, while the tag check read the tag's day in the tagger's timezone, the day before, and reported a release date after the tag. The tag check now reads the tag's UTC day, the clock the preparation uses; a release dated after that day is still refused. `tests/test_preflight.py` gains the case (7 cases): 22:30 at -03:00 with the next day's date passes, two days later is refused.
- The model install's progress lines could go back (17.55 MB, then 16.54 MB of the same file), which failed `tests/test_modelpull.py` inside the gate. Over xet, `huggingface_hub` drives two bars per file with the progress class it is given, the reconstructed bytes and the fewer transferred (compressed) bytes, each from zero, and each instance printed its own count. The instances now share what was shown, so the lines never go back, and a count is capped at the file's size. A new no-network case drives both bars and reproduces the old 16.54 MB line.
- A refused tag could still release and publish. `tagverify.yml` ran as a dist plan job; when it fails, the builds that need it are skipped, and dist's host job runs on skipped builds (`always()`, "skipped is fine"), so the GitHub release and the publish jobs (crates.io builds from source) would have gone ahead. No tag has hit it: every release tag so far was signed. The check now runs as the first job of `wheelmake.yml`, whose failure the host condition refuses.

## [0.5.3] - 2026-10-03

The 0.5.2 release for every channel. The `v0.5.2` tag reached PyPI and nothing else: the release's global job stopped on the payload stamp (below), so the GitHub release, the archives, crates.io, npm and Homebrew never ran. 0.5.3 is 0.5.2 plus that fix; the wheel is built from the same sources. No format or API change.

### Fixed

- The release's global job failed on the 0.5.2 tag before building anything: `scripts/stage_embedder_payload.py` imported `tomllib` (Python 3.11+) to stamp `VERSION`, and dist runs it with the runner's `python3`, 3.10 on ubuntu-22.04. The GitHub release, crates.io, npm and Homebrew jobs were skipped; only the PyPI wheels (their own workflow, which does not stage the payload) went out. 0.5.3 ships the fix; the stamp now falls back to reading `version` from `[workspace.package]` directly; staged under Python 3.10 it writes the same `VERSION`, and `tests/test_embedder_payload.py` covers it with and without `tomllib`.

## [0.5.2] - 2026-10-03

Query any corpus an installed Urna can embed: a sentence-transformers corpus outside the registry (the pt-BR MiniLM and MPNet files), a registry model the catalog installs with `urna setup --model`, and siglip2 offline from its pinned hub snapshot. No format change. No API compatibility break, according to `cargo-semver-checks` against v0.5.1; `urna-runtime` gains public additions (`search_routed`, `ann_ef_floor`, `apply_blob_span_overlay`). A patch release by the project's 0.x policy, where the minor marks a compatibility break: a dependent on `^0.5` takes it with `cargo update`, and the new CLI options and the model installer are additions. Published on PyPI only; the other channels ship as 0.5.3.

### Added

- An installed urna queries a sentence-transformers corpus, task 1 of issue #262. The payload carries `urna/embed_query.py`, `model_fingerprint.py` and a `VERSION` stamp beside `forge/`, and setup lays them all down; it replaces a payload of another release, or one without the stamp, in its own data dir. The MiniLM is the `st_text` preset `minilm-multilingual`, which shares `embed_query.py`'s load, encode and fingerprint, so older corpora keep their `model_hash`. `installed_script_in` takes a path under `python/`, so `search-text` resolves through the data roots; embed failures are typed (`cmd/embed_failure.rs`). The payload install is one restorable step: `cmd::payload::REQUIRED` (every shipped file) is checked first, the previous payload is moved aside to `<root>/.urna-previous-*` and restored on failure (and named in the error when the restore itself fails), and `VERSION` is written last; setup repairs a payload missing a required file. The benchmark rebuild is atomic (`_bench_runner`) and keeps the temporary of a run still building.
- Optional model install, task 2 of issue #262. A corpus built with a model the potion route does not cover needed its packages and weights put in place by hand. The registry now carries an `InstallSpec` for each preset whose install was validated end to end: the hub revision, the exact files fetched at it and the `model_hash` they fingerprint to (the file list matters: the fingerprint hashes every relevant file present, and the MiniLM repo also holds a `pytorch_model.bin` and ONNX exports the pt-BR corpora were never built with). `python/forge/model_catalog.py` turns the registry into `python/forge/catalog.json`, checked in and shipped in the payload: today `minilm-multilingual`, and the eight presets it leaves out, each with its reason (heavy, model-repo code not reviewed and pinned, no pinned hub revision, bundled). `urna setup` gained a models step: `m` opens a picker over the catalog, `--model NAME` (repeatable, `all`) chooses with `--yes`, `--allow-remote-code NAME` is the separate consent for a model's repo code, and a failed model exits `15`. The explorer's ask tab opens an install panel when a query fails for missing packages or weights on a model the catalog offers: what it installs, downloads and must hash to, `y` to install, `r` for repo code, `n` to decline, progress in the panel, the query run again on success. Both run one install (`tui/setup/models.rs`): packages only into the venv setup manages (setup builds it for chosen models even when another Python has numpy and tokenizers; an `URNA_PYTHON` pin elsewhere is refused), then `forge/install_model.py` fetches the pinned files into the shared Hugging Face cache with `URNA_ALLOW_DOWNLOAD=1` for that child only, points `refs/main` at the pin when the cache has none (never moves another revision's), reports progress per megabyte through the hub client (it serves weights over xet, which writes no partial file to watch), and refuses any `model_hash` but the catalog's and the corpus's. `--uninstall` leaves the Hugging Face cache alone. Validated outside the checkout with a release binary, an isolated home and a `file://` release of the staged payload: declining installs nothing; with the hub unreachable the packages install, the fetch fails in the panel with the cause and a retry; accepting downloads the 480 MB at revision `e8f8c211`, exactly its ten files, and the query runs again (top hit 0.520, as `search-text` gives). `install-test.yml` checks every channel's payload carries the catalog (`--model wemm-4b` is refused with its reason, nothing downloaded). Two Python suites join the gate (`test_model_catalog.py`, `test_model_install.py`, which fetches an 18 MB hub test model and skips that case when huggingface.co does not answer). No change to the v1 format, `urna-format`, `urna-runtime`, the wheel or Docker.
- `CITATION.cff` (citation file format 1.2.0) at the root: the author with ORCID, the MIT license, the current release and its crates.io artifact. GitHub shows "Cite this repository" from it and Zenodo and reference managers read the same file. `version` and `date-released` move with every tag.

### Changed

- The Docker image builds the engine-only CLI: `cargo build --locked --no-default-features` from `rust:1.98.1-bookworm` pinned by digest. It used to build the default features, so the image carried the terminal UI (`setup` cannot run in `scratch`, which has no `curl` and no Python), resolved dependencies afresh instead of from `Cargo.lock`, and took whatever Rust 1.x the tag pointed at. The image is 1.0 MB, `validate` and `search` run in it, `setup` is an unknown subcommand. The `--help` footer of a build without the `tui` feature no longer lists `setup` and `tui` or says to run `urna setup` (a unit test covers both builds).
- Every package ships the MIT text: the crates (`crates/*/LICENSE`, linked to `docs/LICENSE`), the wheel (`license-files`), and the five release archives and the npm package (`include` in the CLI's dist metadata; `dist plan` lists `LICENSE` beside `README.md`). The package descriptions read as sentences.
- The README header image links to the documentation site, https://docs.urna.dev/. The two public benchmarks, fakenews-ptbr-urna-benchmark and mtg-urna-benchmark, link their header image there too, on GitHub and on Hugging Face.
- The crate `urna`, the `urna` wheel, the README and `llms.txt` point at the documentation site, https://docs.urna.dev. crates.io showed no documentation link for the binary (docs.rs has nothing to say about it), PyPI listed only the repository and the issues, and the README never named the site; search engines and package pages had no path from the package to the docs. The crate gets `documentation`, the wheel gets `Homepage` and `Documentation` in `[project.urls]`; both reach the registries with the next release.
- `docs/SECURITY.md` supports 0.5.x; the table said 0.3.x was current two minor versions after 0.3 shipped.
- The docs describe the hashes as the layout has them: the header and section checksums are the first 8 bytes of a SHA-256, only `file_hash` and `content_hash` are full `sha256:<64 hex>` strings, the footer digest is over everything before it while the reported `file_hash` covers the whole file (SECURITY.md, README.md, ARC.toml, usage sections 2 and 4, which also lists the `blobs` and `spaces` keys `inspect --json` emits).
- The HNSW beam floor is visible. `search_ann(q, k, ef)` always searched `max(ef, k, ef_construction)` candidates (the floor keeps the recall measured at build), so `--ef 100` on a Python-built file ran a 400-wide beam and the `ANN ef=100` line in `benchmark` read as a 100-wide search. `MmapUrnaFile::ann_ef_floor` exposes the floor, every CLI result prints a `candidates:` line (route, per-path counts, fusion, rerank source), `benchmark` prints the beam next to the ef, and the `--ef` help, `docs/USAGE.md` and the README say it. The default is unchanged.
- `search-text` and `doctor` run Python, and the docs say so: `urna --help`, the CLI docstring, README.md, usage's intro and Docker note and ARC.toml all said the engine verbs never do.
- Every doc starts with a YAML header (`project`, `audience`, `status`, `last-updated`, `domain`): usage, bench (its generator emits it), changelog, security, contributing, code of conduct, the demo instructions, the example and fuzz READMEs, the vendored model and demo corpus READMEs. Exempt: the README (packaged by the registries), the license, the PR template, llms.txt and the demo corpus documents. `llms.txt` follows the llms.txt format now (title, summary, links with a line each) and points at the docs.
- One interpreter ladder and one script ladder, stated the same way in usage section 5 and the offline notes (section 11 already had them right): `URNA_PYTHON`, the setup venv, the nearest `.venv` up from the CWD, `python3`; the repo layout, then `<root>/urna/forge/` for each of the five data roots. Section 5 skipped the setup venv, the offline notes listed two roots of five.
- Usage sections 12 to 14 describe the spec the code reads: the flags `urna build` forwards and the three (`--strict-env`, `--seed`, `--json`) that exist only on `urna_forge.py` (section 13 documented `--strict-env` on `urna build`, a clap usage error); the `[build]` table (`preset` defaults to `hybrid`, `with_graph` to true, `mrl_dim` has no ladder check), the `corpus`, `source`, `models` and `media` keys the code reads and the doc did not, and that the profile name lands in the lock, not the manifest (the `--rebuild-only` and `--resume` wording of the L3 paragraph rides with #258, which changes that behavior); section 14's jxl-transcode ratio is 1.10x, not "~20% smaller"; the registry table's MRL column shows the discrete ladders instead of ranges and stray `;` cells.
- Usage section 1 describes the standalone image tool as what it is: `urna_build_image_corpus.py` writes `corpus.urna` next to a `corpus.media/` sidecar and `corpus.manifest.json`, with the image vectors as the default space and `retrieve` queries; the in-file media sections (0x14, 0x16, 0x17) and the named spaces (0x15 + bands) it was credited with are written by the declarative build only, which the section now points at. The sweep example parses (`--variants "av1-intra:35,40;dtype:int8"`). ARC.toml's `image-corpus-tooling` role and the `image_corpus.py` entry say the same.
- `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md` describes the tree as it is: the kebab-case naming rule, the CLI crate's 1.88 floor next to the workspace 1.85 (usage's dev build note and ARC.toml's `Cargo.toml` purpose say the same), the extension built with `pyo3/extension-module` (the build the old text gave segfaults under uv's standalone Python), what the gate runs and what CI adds and does not run (no Python suites), and the private advisory form next to the email.
- Homebrew is documented as `brew tap hoffresearch/urna` then `brew install urna` (and `brew upgrade urna`); the one-line `brew install hoffresearch/urna/urna` still works. A bare `brew install urna` without the tap needs the formula in homebrew-core, which asks for 75 stars and a build from source.
- The release verification docs say what v0.5.1 carries: a `.sha256` on the five archives and the payload (not the SBOM, the formula or the npm package), a build attestation on the five archives only (the payload and the SBOM are built in the global job; `gh attestation verify urna.cdx.xml` answered 404 and is gone from the doc), no PEP 740 on any wheel yet, and install-test as its three jobs (`cli`, `channels` with eleven legs, `wheel`). Usage, SECURITY.md and ARC.toml agree.
- The README's image table names the forge's profiles: `stills` is AVIF q48 (1.20 GB, 3.32x on the 38627 cards, the 2026-09-12 measurement), `stills-av1` is the 1.37 GB all-intra stream the row used to call `stills`; the link to the private benchmark repository is gone (a 404 for every reader), the sentence says the code and data are not published yet and where the measurements are recorded.
- bun, pnpm and yarn install `@urna/cli` like npm does (`bun add -g`, `pnpm add -g`, `yarn global add`), now in the README, `docs/USAGE.md` and `install-test.yml` (bun and pnpm on Linux and macOS, yarn 1 on Linux). bun and pnpm 10 block the package's postinstall; the `urna` wrapper downloads the binary on the first run, so nothing has to be allowed.
- The npm section of usage says the `urna` command of npm, bun, pnpm and yarn is a Node launcher: it needs Node on PATH (exit 127 without it, `bunx --bun @urna/cli` runs it under bun) and costs about 40 ms per call against 3 ms for the binary; `install-test.yml` records that its bun legs pass because the runners ship Node, and why no leg asserts the no-node case.
- `install-test.yml` runs inside every release, as a dist post-announce job. It listened for `release: published`, which never fired: dist creates the release with `GITHUB_TOKEN`, and events from that token start no workflows, so 0.5.0 and 0.5.1 were tested by hand. Its wheel job now installs the tag's version (waiting for `pypi.yml`, a separate run) instead of whatever was newest on PyPI.

### Removed

- The pt-BR corpus build: `python/tools/urna_build_corpus.py` and `_corpus_sources.py`, whose upstream CSVs and one mirror no longer exist, so they could not run; the python-corpus-build flow and `data/measure/baseline.json` went with them. The pinned, rebuildable corpus lives in the fakenews-ptbr-urna-benchmark dataset. No code, format or gate change.

### Fixed

- `scripts/release_check.sh` failed on a fresh checkout: it built `python/_urna.so` after `cargo test`, and the CLI end-to-end tests (`cli_e2e.rs`, two cases) build their demo corpus through `python/urna.py`, so they failed with `Cannot find _urna extension` before the gate reached the step that would have built it. A checkout that had run the gate once never saw it. The extension is built right after the release build now.
- The `siglip2` text tower could not run offline. `embed_image.py` asked open_clip for the tokenizer by name; transformers' AutoTokenizer then looked up `config.json`, which `timm/ViT-B-16-SigLIP2` does not have, without a commit hash, and failed with `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` even with every file cached (by name it fails with a pinned revision and with `local_files_only` too; from the snapshot directory it works). `ask` and `retrieve` on a SigLIP2 corpus failed the same way; the old workaround, turning offline off, hid it. The preset now pins `revision = eee10eff` and its five files, and loads both the weights and the tokenizer from `snapshots/<revision>`, never `refs/main` (a cache fetched by commit hash has no `refs/main`, and open_clip by tag asks for `main`). The checkpoint goes into open_clip's built-in architecture with the `webli` tag's preprocess, so the `model_hash` stays sha256:a9946db1..., the hash of the SigLIP2 space in the mtg-urna-benchmark release v0.3/stills-5models; `local-dir:` is used for the tokenizer only, because it builds the preprocess from JSON and the lists repr differently from the tag's tuples, which would have moved the hash. The tokenizer is outside the fingerprint; its ids match the load by name on 2000 card labels. A missing snapshot file is `SnapshotMissing`, naming the file and the `hf download ... --revision` line, exit 3 with `urna-fetch:` from `embed_query_model.py`; `URNA_ALLOW_DOWNLOAD=1` fetches the pinned files. The preset requires `transformers` (its tokenizer imports it). Measured with the network blocked (`sandbox-exec` deny network) on a cache holding only the snapshot: the mtg benchmark's evaluator ran 20 SigLIP2 queries on stills-5models, model_hash and file checks passed, hit@1 0.65 (the full-corpus 0.750 is inside its 0.45 to 0.85 interval). Tests: `test_model_registry.py` (the pin, the revision over `refs/main`, a missing file named) and `python/forge/test_open_clip_snapshot.py` (new: the query offline from the snapshot alone with the build's model_hash, and a missing tokenizer file, exit 3). `clip-vit-b32` is unchanged and still loads by tag (#271).
- The #271 merge into a `main` that had gained `InstallSpec` (#268) kept the `ModelPreset` dataclass of `main` and dropped the four snapshot fields (`hf_repo`, `revision`, `weights_file`, `snapshot_files`) while the SigLIP2 preset and `pinned_snapshot` still used them: `forge/model_registry.py` raised `TypeError` at import, so every registry user failed (forge builds, `ask` and `retrieve` on a registry model, the catalog, the payload stage). It also kept the docs of `main`, losing the #271 changelog entry and leaving the old SigLIP2 known limitation in usage section 12. The fields and docs are restored, the catalog says why SigLIP2 is not offered (the installer verifies a file fingerprint; an open_clip `model_hash` hashes the loaded tensors), and CI's python job runs `python python/forge/model_catalog.py --check`, which imports the whole registry under a bare python: ruff alone passed the broken tree.
- `search-text` on an MRL-truncated corpus failed the dim layer of the gate: `ask` and `retrieve` passed `--mrl-dim` to their embedder when the manifest records `full_dim`, `search-text` passed nothing and `python/embed_query.py` had no such flag. Both now do (`embed_query.slice_renorm`, the same truncate-then-renormalize the builder and the registry apply); case 6 of `test_search_text_model_hash.py` and case 5 of `test_query_embedder_routing.py`.
- `--skip-model-hash-check` did what its name says and skipped the model_hash comparison entirely, while its help and the gate's own docstring said it was for the legacy placeholder only. The code follows the contract now: the flag accepts a placeholder corpus (no fingerprint to compare), a real fingerprint that disagrees with the embedder is never skipped, and the error names the flag's scope (case 6 of `test_search_text_model_hash.py`).
- `urna.build` refuses the zero `model_hash` placeholder at write time, as `docs/USAGE.md` had claimed for a while; the refusal names `emb.model_hash()`. `allow_placeholder_model_hash=True` (also on `BuildConfig`) keeps the legacy placeholder for test fixtures and synthetic benchmarks; the Rust `UrnaFileBuilder` and the byte-frozen golden fixture are unchanged, and the CLI gate still refuses a placeholder corpus at query time. `test_builder.py` covers the refusal, the pass-through of the pipeline, a real hash and the opt-in.
- `scripts/release_check.sh` failed its own `cargo test` step whenever `URNA_PYTHON` was exported, which is how its header says to run it: two `urna setup` plan tests read the variable from the process env and planned around a pinned interpreter the fixture never declared. The scan now carries the pin (`Scan::pinned_python`), `plan` is a pure function of the scan again, and a third test covers the pinned case (blocked with the variable named while the deps are missing, nothing to do once they are there).
- `source.kind = "pdf_dir"` passed spec validation and failed at row loading every time (`build via forge_pipeline (pages are temporary)`, thrown by forge_pipeline itself). Validation refuses it and names `python/tools/urna_build_image_corpus.py --pdf` and the `image_dir` kind; the dead branch is gone from `corpus_sources.py`, the quickstart README lists the four kinds.
- `urna cite` on a media corpus printed the row ordinal the forge stores in 0x03 while search hits and `retrieve` printed the blob URI and byte range from the 0x16 span overlay, so the same citation resolved to two spans. cite now applies the overlay through `urna_runtime::apply_blob_span_overlay`, the same code `MmapUrnaFile::open` runs, without opening the indices. `crates/urna-cli/tests/media_cite_validate.rs` builds a real media corpus and checks the blob chunk, and a text chunk (`BLOB_REF_NONE`) that keeps its stored span.
- `urna validate` printed `OK:` and six check lines before it verified the inlined blobs (0x17), so a blob failing its `content_hash` left an `OK:` on stdout and exit 1. Every check now runs before the first line; a failing blob prints nothing on stdout. Covered by the same test file, with a file whose checksums are right and whose recorded blob hash is not.
- `urna build --rebuild-only` compared the rebuilt lock with the one on disk and then wrote the new lock over it, so a divergence erased the record it had just been compared against. The lock on disk is the record of the original build and is never rewritten by a rebuild: a match leaves it byte-identical, a divergence writes `<name>.build.lock.rebuild.json` beside it (and `rebuild_lock` in the result), and `--strict-env` still refuses before anything is written. `test_forge_spec.py::test_rebuild_only_keeps_the_lock` forces a real divergence through `URNA_ST_DEVICE`.
- `crf = "auto"` crashed with `AttributeError: 'Row' object has no attribute 'text'` on any row without a label (an `image_dir` without a labels file, an empty `label_template`): the gate's utility queries now fall back to the row's canonical text (`forge_media_stage.gate_labels`, tested in `test_quality_gate.py`).
- The installed binary can ask a wemm, CLIP or Jina corpus: the release payload carries the registry query embedder (`forge/embed_query_model.py`, the registry, its adapters, the two model backends and `model_fingerprint.py`) next to the potion route. It does not carry torch or sentence-transformers; a registry model now fails with the exact `pip install` line for the setup venv, exit 4, where before the failure was `embedder script not found`. `tests/test_embedder_payload.py` stages the payload and runs both embedders from outside the checkout; `release_check.sh` runs it as the ninth suite.
- `urna setup` no longer ends by recommending `urna build --spec corpus.toml` to an installed binary that cannot run it (the forge is not in any release artifact): the success screen and the plain `--yes` log say the build runs from a checkout of the repo, and the `build` launcher's error names the clone instead of a "forge payload" that never existed.
- `ask`, `retrieve` and `search-text` route by what the file carries, not by the manifest's `index_type`. The `hybrid` preset writes `index_type = "hnsw"` and marks BM25 as the `supports_bm25` capability (`urna.build` never called the builder's `.hybrid()`), so the string route sent every Python-built hybrid file down the HNSW path and its BM25 section was read only by an explicit `search_hybrid` call; the quickstart corpus and every forge default build were in that state. `MmapUrnaFile::search_routed` is the one route the three verbs and the Python `UrnaFile.retrieve` share: BM25 section plus query text gives the hybrid path, an HNSW section gives ann, otherwise exact; the graph is reached only through `search-graph`. `UrnaFile.retrieve` gains `query_text=` (the vector carries no words; `forge/retrieve.py` passes it), and its `hops` argument is kept, unused. New `crates/urna-runtime/tests/search_routed.rs` and a `test_e2e.py` case where a chunk sitting at cosine -1 reaches the rerank through its one word.

## [0.5.1] - 2026-09-26

The same binaries as 0.5.0, republished so every registry page shows install commands that work: the 0.5.0 pages on crates.io and npm say `npm install -g urna`, the PyPI page says `cargo install urna-cli`, and neither exists. It is also the first tag whose npm package CI publishes itself.

### Changed

- Doc names in uppercase like the other top-level docs: `docs/BENCH.md` (was `benchmarks.md`), `docs/USAGE.md` (was `usage.md`), `docs/arc/ARC.toml` (was `arc.toml`). Every live pointer follows; the entries below keep the names they had.
- npm is `@urna/cli` in the config again: npm refused the bare `urna` (below), so `npm-scope` / `npm-package` point at the name 0.5.0 actually shipped under, and the next tag publishes it from CI.
- `install-test.yml` runs the x86_64 Mac leg on `macos-15-intel`: GitHub retired the `macos-13` runners and that job queued forever.
- `pillow` joins the `forge` dependency group in `pyproject.toml`: the image paths and the forge tests import it, and it was installed by hand.
- The release lessons of 0.5.0 are written down: item 11 of the maintainer checklist in `docs/USAGE.md` (LFS, dist, moving a tag, the PyPI run, npm naming, runner labels).

### Fixed

- The Windows one-liner: dist's Windows zip holds `urna.exe` at its root, and `install.ps1` copied `urna-<target>\urna.exe`, so it failed on the first release that shipped a Windows archive. It now takes `urna.exe` wherever the zip puts it. `install.ps1` is served from `main`, so the fix reaches 0.5.0 installs with no new tag.

## [0.5.0] - 2026-09-26

The project is `urna` now, and the first tag the release pipeline serves: CLI archives on the GitHub release, the Homebrew tap, npm `@urna/cli`, crates.io (`urna-format`, `urna-runtime`, `urna`) and the PyPI wheel `urna`. No format change; a `.nest` from 0.4.0 still opens.

### Added

- `urna setup`, the installer every channel ends in (2026-09-26). Homebrew, npm and crates.io ship the bare binary, and a bare binary exits `4` from `urna doctor`: it cannot carry the ~30 MB embedder payload or a Python with numpy and tokenizers. Setup lays both down and proves the result. On a terminal it runs inline in four steps (scan the machine, a checkbox plan, install, verify), with the last screen left in the scrollback; with `--yes`, or without a terminal, it prints plain lines from the same worker. The payload comes from the release matching the binary's version through a system `curl` child process (HTTPS only, no downgrade on redirect; the binary still links no network stack), its SHA256 is checked while it streams, and it is unpacked into a staging dir that aborts on any entry escaping it, then swapped in for `<data root>/urna/forge`. A failed download or a checksum mismatch leaves the previous payload untouched. The Python env is `<data root>/urna/venv`, built with uv when it is on `PATH`, else `python3 -m venv` + pip. New exit codes above doctor's: `10` download, `11` checksum, `12` unpack, `13` Python env, `14` a step the machine needs is blocked (no curl, no uv and no python3, no data dir, or `URNA_PYTHON` pinning an interpreter without the deps). `--version`, `--force`, `--no-payload`, `--no-python`, `--uninstall` (removes the payload and the env, never the binary). Measured on an M-series Mac from a `file://` release, release build, whole command including the scan and the checks: 1.2 s for the 28.4 MB payload (download, SHA256, unpack), 2.0 s for the env with uv on a warm cache.
- `urna tui`, the corpus explorer, also what a bare `urna` opens on a terminal (in a pipe a bare `urna` prints the help and exits `2`, as before). Home (the symbol over a rain of hex glyphs, the `.urna` files found in the working dir and one level below), corpus (the manifest, the verdict of the checks `urna validate` runs, the section table with a size bar per section), ask (the same offline embedder and `model_hash` gate as `urna ask`, hits with the exact-rerank score as a bar, the stored text with its citation and source, and the error turned into the fix when the embedder is missing), health (the doctor checks, re-runnable), a file picker, toasts, mouse on tabs and scroll. `s` hands the terminal to `urna setup` and comes back with the same corpus open. About 2 to 3 % of one core with the rain running on a 160x48 terminal, 34 MB resident.
- The look is urna.dev's: the site's palette (`constants/themes.ts`: ground `#002b36`, ink `#dfe8ea`, the title teal, the benchmark thermometer) and its symbol and wordmark rasterized to braille. Every screen change is a tachyonfx effect. 24-bit color where the terminal advertises it, the nearest xterm-256 color elsewhere (Terminal.app, tmux without `Tc`; the ground folds to the grey Solarized itself falls back to, not to navy), nothing under `NO_COLOR`; `URNA_COLOR=truecolor|256|none` overrides. `urna doctor` got the same palette on a terminal and stays plain when piped.
- Built on Ratatui 0.30, tachyonfx 0.25, ratatui-explorer (the picker), ratatui-cheese (spinners, the key help, the query input), hyperrat (links), tui-scrollbar and coolor (the 256-color fold); adapted, with attribution in each file: the panel/gauge/checklist vocabulary of noble, the hash rain from tui-rain, the checkbox from tui-checkbox and the toasts from ratatui-toaster (the last three are on Ratatui 0.29 or need tokio). All of it sits behind the default-on `tui` feature: `cargo install urna --no-default-features` builds the engine-only CLI, 2.1 MB against 3.9 MB for the full binary (release, aarch64 macOS). The sovereign workspace suite is 434 Rust tests (371 before): the terminal UI's unit tests (palette fold, effects, rain, curl download over `file://`, staged unpack with a traversal case, the plan, key and mouse routing, the corpus and ask views) and `tests/setup_e2e.rs`, the real binary against a `file://` release.

### Changed

- The header mark of `urna setup` and `urna tui` is the Urna symbol (2026-09-26): a three-row braille drawing of `logo-symbol.svg` (the horns flaring out, the teardrop slit, the full base), drawn dot by dot on the SVG's coverage at 10x12 because a straight raster loses the horns at that size; it replaces a fragment of the small symbol that did not read as the logo. The header grows to three rows (mark, title and version or the open corpus, the stepper or the tabs, a rule) and falls back to one plain row on a terminal under 20 rows. The explorer's hit list now scrolls to keep the selected hit on screen (the tenth of ten was selectable but drawn off the panel).
- Images live in `assets/images/` (2026-09-26): the new dark header (`urna-hoff-research-db-iage-thumb-git.png`, the GitHub layout) replaces the old light one at the top of the README, next to its social thumbnail variant and the setup / TUI screenshots; `docs/` keeps text. Every README image is a raw `main` URL, so the page renders the same on GitHub, crates.io and PyPI.
- `urna-cli` moves to Rust 1.88 (Ratatui 0.30's floor) with its own `rust-version`; `urna-format`, `urna-runtime` and `urna-python` keep the workspace 1.85 (2026-09-26).
- One data-root ladder (2026-09-26, `cmd/paths.rs`): the CLI now searches `URNA_DATA_DIR`, `XDG_DATA_HOME`, `~/.local/share`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%` and `<exe>/../share` for the payload. Fixed on the way: `install.ps1` lays the payload down under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\urna`, a directory the CLI never searched, so a Windows one-liner install could not find its own embedder. The interpreter ladder gains the setup venv right after `URNA_PYTHON`, ahead of the walk-up `.venv` heuristic, so an installed Urna run from inside another project's `.venv` still embeds with its own env.
- The doctor checks are data (`cmd/health.rs`), shared by `doctor`, setup's verify step and the explorer; `doctor`'s output, stop points and exit codes are unchanged, the install failures now name `urna setup`, and a missing embedder says where it looked instead of printing a relative fallback path.
- `install-test.yml` gains a job per package channel (Homebrew on macOS and Linux, npm on Linux, macOS and Windows, cargo binstall) that ends in `urna setup --yes` and `urna doctor`, and its checks run from outside the checkout: inside it the embedder resolved to the repo's `python/forge`, whose potion table is an LFS pointer on the runner. `ci.yml` gains a Windows job for the CLI (Clippy + unit tests + the setup end to end) and a Clippy pass without the `tui` feature. The one-liners end by pointing at `urna setup` and `urna tui`.

- PyPI bootstrap (2026-09-26): `pypi.yml` passes the `pypi` environment's `PYPI_API_TOKEN` secret as the publish password. A trusted publisher can only be attached to a project that exists, so the first publish needs a token; with the secret set the action skips the PEP 740 attestations, and with it deleted the empty password selects OIDC trusted publishing again, no workflow change. 0.5.0 is the release published with the token.

- Registry channels configured, nothing published yet (2026-09-26): the release now feeds crates.io and npm next to Homebrew. `urna-format`, `urna-runtime` and `urna-cli` carry the crates.io metadata (description, README, keywords, categories, repository) and `.github/workflows/publish-crates.yml`, a cargo-dist custom publish job, publishes them in dependency order on the tag and skips a version already on crates.io, so a re-run finishes a half-done release; `urna-python` and `forge-core` are `publish = false` (the wheel is the Python channel; `forge-core` is taken on crates.io by an unrelated project). The workspace dependencies on `urna-format` / `urna-runtime` carry `version` next to `path`, which is what crates.io resolves, so a release bump moves both (maintainer checklist step 8). cargo-dist gains the npm installer (`@urna/cli`, binary `urna`) and the Homebrew formula is renamed from `urna-cli` to `urna`, matching the `brew install hoffresearch/urna/urna` the docs already promised (the generated name would have been `hoffresearch/urna/urna-cli`). `release.yml` regenerated with `dist generate`. On the account side: the `hoffresearch/homebrew-urna` tap exists with an empty `Formula/`, and the `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` and `NPM_TOKEN` repo secrets are set; `HOMEBREW_TAP_TOKEN` is a fine-grained token scoped to the tap, expiring 2027-09-26. A follow-up grants the crates publish job `contents: read` through `github-custom-job-permissions`: the custom job got `id-token` + `packages` only, which zeroed `contents`, so the Release workflow failed at startup. `cargo publish --workspace --dry-run` packages and verifies all three crates.
- `doc/` is `docs/` and `dat/` is `data/` (2026-09-22): every path in the README, llms.txt, arc.toml, the gitignore/gitattributes/dockerignore, `release_check.sh` (`URNA_BASELINE` default), the pre-commit allow-list, the Dockerfile run line, the measure baselines' `baseline_path`, and the Rust/Python comments follows. Directory rename only, no format or runtime change.
- A quickstart that runs from a clean checkout (2026-09-22): `examples/quickstart/` carries twelve CC0 paragraphs as `docs.jsonl`, the smallest `corpus.toml` that builds them (jsonl source, potion), and `quickstart.py`, the same loop on the Python surface with the embedder and `model_hash` in plain sight. The README opens with the five-verb table (build creates the base, ask queries it, retrieve hands cited spans to another program, cite resolves the source, validate proves the file) and `urna --help` prints the same block under `start here`. The README's Python snippet now shows where `qvec` and the embedder come from, and usage §1's build example no longer passes the all-zero `model_hash` the writer rejects.
- ruff isort first-party set declared in `pyproject.toml` (2026-09-22): `urna`, `builder`, `forge`, `tools` and the other `python/` modules. ruff was inferring the split from which directories exist beside `pyproject.toml`, so a local `tools/` folder at the root turned `from tools import` into a first-party import and `I001` fired on one machine and not in CI. Eleven files got the resulting blank line between the third-party and the first-party block; order inside blocks is unchanged.
- CI: `actions/cache@v6` for the fuzz-soak corpus (v4 ran on Node 20, which the runners now force onto Node 24 with a deprecation annotation on each of the four soak jobs), and `ubuntu-latest` pinned to `ubuntu-24.04` in ci, tag-verify, pypi and install-test (the label migrates to Ubuntu 26 on 2026-10-19; release.yml already pins 22.04). No change to what the jobs run.
- The project is now `urna` (2026-09-17): repository `hoffresearch/urna`, crates `urna-format` / `urna-runtime` / `urna-cli` / `urna-python`, CLI binary `urna`, Python module `urna` (extension `_urna`), PyPI wheel `urna`, Homebrew tap `hoffresearch/homebrew-urna`, env vars `URNA_*`, citation scheme `urna://`, file extension `.urna`. The file magic is now `URNA` (`0x55 0x52 0x4E 0x41`) and the chunk-id domain is `urna:chunk_id:v1`, the reader also accepts the legacy `NEST` magic, so a `.nest` written by 0.4.0 or earlier still opens, validates and searches under `urna` (`layout::LEGACY_MAGIC`, `tests/legacy_magic.rs` on the 0.4.0 golden bytes; 371 Rust tests in the sovereign workspace now); the writer only emits `URNA`. Chunk ids of a legacy file were derived under the old domain and are not reproduced by `urna.chunk_id` on the same text. The container layout, section ids, encodings and manifest schema are otherwise unchanged (format v1, schema v1). The golden fixture and the LFS demo corpus were regenerated through the builder from the same chunks and embeddings (same section sizes byte for byte; only chunk ids, magic and hashes differ).
- `docs/arc` consolidated into a single `docs/arc/arc.toml` (2026-09-18): merged the former `arc.yaml` (machine and human architecture reference), `arc.mmd` (Mermaid visual map), and `arc.schema.json` (the IDE-validation schema for `arc.yaml`) into one file. The Mermaid source lives byte-identical under `diagram.source`; the former JSON-schema binding (a yaml-language-server modeline) has no TOML equivalent, so the shape is now documented in a `schema` table instead of IDE-enforced. Doc-location change only, no architecture, format, or runtime change.

### Fixed

- The release pipeline, as the 0.5.0 tag found it (five runs, 2026-09-26). The repository ran out of its git-lfs budget, and the release jobs ran a bare `git lfs pull` (150 MB of demo corpora on each of five targets, for a 30 MB table): `scripts/fetch_potion.sh` now downloads the potion table from `minishlab/potion-base-8M` at the pinned revision and accepts it only when its SHA256 is the oid in the LFS pointer, and the payload build calls it itself because dist's global job does not run `github-build-setup`. dist built `--workspace` per target, and `urna-python`'s cdylib cannot build for musl: `precise-builds = true`. dist's `source.tar.gz` is a `git archive` that smudges every LFS file: `source-tarball = false` (GitHub attaches the source to every release). The CLI crate publishes as `urna` (`cargo install urna`). npm refused the bare name `urna` (`403 package name too similar to existing packages url, urix, lerna, ora`), so 0.5.0 went to npm as `@urna/cli`, published from the release's own npm package.

## [0.4.0] - 2026-09-17

> Note (2026-09-26): `v0.4.0` was never tagged, so the "first tag served by the release pipeline" below did not happen; 0.5.0 is the first.

The forge as a product: declarative builds, media inside the file, several image models per file, a quality gate with a task-utility floor, and the MTG card corpus benchmark that drove most of it (twenty experiments, [brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark](https://github.com/brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark)). First tag served by the release pipeline.

### Changed

- Compact manifest items under `provenance = "minimal"` (2026-09-12): the forge manifest of a 38627-item corpus was 13 to 16 MB because `items[]` carried `image_path`, `key`, `label`, `media_uri` and `ordinal` per item (12 MB of the file). `minimal` now writes `key` + `ordinal` per item and a top-level `items_compact = true`; `image_path` and `label` are the redacted source path and the text label, `media_uri` is derived from the ordinal, the dedup map and the media block (`forge_manifest.frame_resolver`; the map lands as a top-level `frame_of_row` only when dedup collapsed rows, since nothing else in the manifest records it). `standard` and `full` keep the full items and write `items_compact = false`. Readers go through `forge_manifest.manifest_items(manifest, need=(...))`, which raises a clear error naming `provenance = "standard"` when a caller needs a dropped field: `urna_model_bench.py` (source-image queries need `image_path`) and `urna_ui_bridge.py` (browse titles fall back to the key; the frame comes from the resolver, which also fixes the browse frame of a sharded build: the old parse took the shard-local frame as global). `print_table` moves to `python/tools/_model_bench_report.py` (bench was 322 lines, now 286). `tests/test_forge_spec.py` gains `test_provenance_minimal`: compact items carry exactly key and ordinal, `items_compact` true, `frame_of_row` maps the dup rows to one frame, the resolver agrees between the compact and the standard manifest, the `.urna` `file_hash` is identical to the standard build (the manifest is a sidecar), `manifest_items` refuses `image_path` / `label` / `media_uri` with the fixing mode in the message, and the UI bridge browses both manifests to the same blob URI + frame per ordinal; 12/12 pass.
- `tune = "still"` is the AV1 default (2026-09-13): `MediaSpec.tune` in `python/forge/build_spec.py` defaulted to `"default"` while every AV1 media profile already set `still`; measured on 2048 cards at CRF 35, the still tune gives ssimulacra2 p50 62.7 against 51.8 for the default tune for +10% bytes, so the bare default follows the profiles. `"default"` stays an accepted value. This changes the media state key: `asdict(spec.media)` enters the params hash (`forge_media_stage._media_params`), so an existing AV1 build that relied on the implicit default re-encodes its media once on `--resume`; a spec that sets `tune` explicitly is unaffected. `test_forge_spec.py` pins the default and the explicit `"default"`.
- The AVIF backend pins avifenc's worker count (2026-09-13): `-j 8` (`AVIF_JOBS` in `image_encode_still.py`), recorded as `toolchain.params.jobs`. libaom writes different bytes with one worker than with two or more (measured on 256 MTG cards, libavif 1.4.2 / aom 3.15.0: every file differed between `-j 1` and `-j 2`; 2, 3, 4, 8, 16 and `all` were byte-identical, 3.7 kB apart over the set), and avifenc's default is `all`, so the stills profile's file_hash was a function of the build machine's core count. SVT-AV1 (`lp` 1, 2, 8, 16) and cjxl (`--num_threads` 1, 8, default) were byte-identical across worker counts on the same run. New test `test_avif_jobs_are_pinned_and_recorded`.
- Read side measured on a real corpus, three fixes (2026-09-13): `decode_frames_at` bounds FFmpeg to the hit span with `-frames:v` (without it the encoder kept decoding past the last hit, hit the closed pipe once the buffer filled and every batched resolve on the 38627-card stream raised a broken-pipe RuntimeError; the 12-frame test stream hid it because its last hit was the last frame, the test now also asks for a span that stops early); `decode_avif` writes its PNG intermediate with `--png-compress 0` (145 ms to 18 ms per 488x680 card, identical pixels, asserted); `decode_jxl` reads a PPM instead of a PNG (30 ms to 11 ms, lossless output equal to the source pixels, asserted). End to end on the MTG candidates, 200 random cards each, same machine and load: AVIF q48 94 ms to 32 ms p50, jxl-transcode archive 43 ms to 16 ms. The AV1 stream stays at 27 to 36 ms per single seek, 23 ms of which is the FFmpeg process start; the batched path walks every frame between the first and the last hit, so for k random hits it costs more than k seeks (239 ms per frame at 10 hits over 38627 frames) and is only the right call for hits that sit close together.
- The `stills` media profile is one AVIF per image (2026-09-12): `profile = "stills"` resolves to `backend = "avif"`, `crf = 48` (avifenc -q; the AVIF backend has no separate quality key), `speed = 8` instead of the all-intra AV1 stream with the still tune. Measured on 38627 cards (experiment 11 of brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark): libaom at q48 speed 8 writes 1195973116 B against 1374431484 B for the AV1 stills stream, 13% less at matched ssimulacra2 mean 61.96 on the 2048 sample, with O(1) per-image access and no video decode; the cost is build time, the CLIP embed runs 4 to 10x slower because frames are decoded one AVIF at a time. The old recipe stays as `profile = "stills-av1"` (all-intra + still tune) so nothing that relied on the stream is lost; explicit keys still win over either. Two things the switch surfaced: `media.speed` never reached the AVIF encoder (avifenc always ran at its default 8; `image_backends._avif` now passes it, asserted on the manifest record in `test_image_corpus.py`), and `crf = "auto"` on a non-AV1 backend silently applied an AV1 CRF as an avifenc -q, since the gate encodes its ladder with the AV1 stream; it is now a SpecError naming `media.crf` (pair the gate with `stills-av1`). Usage section 13's example spec and the section 14 profile bullet follow. The three MTG release profiles live in the benchmark repository and are unaffected. `test_forge_spec.py` pins the six profiles, the stills resolution, the explicit CRF override and the refused pairing.
- Gate floors a real corpus can reach (2026-09-12): `QualitySpec` defaults move from `visual_floor_p10 = 85`, `visual_floor_min = 72`, `drift_floor_p10 = 0.98`, `crf_ladder = [30, 35, 40, 45]` to `60`, `45`, `0.95`, `[25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 50]`. The old floors were set for large photos; on the MTG cards at 488x680 yuv420 no rung of the ladder reached p10 85 (experiment 02 av1-auto-dualgate, 2048 sample: crf30 gives ssim2 p50 70.6, p10 65.3, min 58.6, drift p10 0.967), so `crf = "auto"` always fell back to the smallest CRF with a warning. The new numbers come from the AV1 still speed 6 points on the card corpus: crf30 (p10 65.3, min 58.6, drift 0.967) passes and crf35 (p50 62.7, p10 55.7, min 45.3, drift p10 0.965) fails on p10, so the default picks a rung instead of always warning; the dataclass comment records the derivation. Each floor and the ladder still override under `[media.quality]` (the old floors are one override away), pinned by `test_quality_defaults` in `test_forge_spec.py`; `test_quality_gate.py` sets every floor explicitly and passes unchanged. Existing gated builds re-encode once on `--resume`: `forge_media_stage._media_params` hashes `asdict(spec.media)`, which includes the nested quality table, so a spec that did not pin the four keys sees a new media state key (a spec that pins all four keeps its key). A `profile = "stills"` build re-encodes for the same reason (the resolved backend changed).
- Forge text-only recipes no longer carry the decoder fingerprint (2026-09-12): `forge_recipe.recipe` adds the `decoder` block only for `image = "space"` models, so a potion recipe hash is the same across media variants. Measured on the three MTG candidates (AVIF q48, AV1 retrieval, AV1 crf50): same `corpus_input_hash`, three different potion recipe hashes, the identical 39.5 MB potion table computed three times. The potion recipe hash changes once for existing builds, which `--rebuild-only --strict-env` reports as a build.lock diff. `forge_pipeline.py` (377 lines) is split: `forge_recipe.py` (recipe, model dir fingerprint, model_hash probe) and `forge_media_stage.py` (the media stage), the pipeline keeps build, dedup and the embed stage at 225 lines.

- README reworked for copy-paste and charts (2026-09-11): every CLI verb and every Python call is its own fenced block inside collapsed `<details>` groups (agent verbs, search family, inspect/validate/stats/cite/media/benchmark/doctor; Python open/retrieve, search variants, validate/inspect, build), the install block is split one command per fence with the dev build collapsed, and the benchmarks section carries the comparatives as Mermaid charts (a quadrant of warm p50 vs p99 per store after the opening text and in `docs/benchmarks.md`, then collapsed: the preset ladder as size ratio vs recall, p50 and p99 bars, cold open and build bars, the MRL recall curve for int8 and int4), every value copied from `docs/benchmarks.md` and `data/measure/ladder.json`. The opening is tighter (the four sovereign properties are a two-column table), and presets, benchmarks, the CLI intro and the reference list (docs, architecture, contracts as three collapsed groups; the architecture section moved there) are collapsed too; the hardening section leaves the README, its one user-facing line (a panic on a malformed file is a security bug) moves under contracts and the rest is already in `docs/SECURITY.md`. No number changed.
- `docs/arc/arc.schema.json`: a draft-07 JSON schema for the architecture reference (header, layers, flows, narrative lists, inventory item shape), with a `yaml-language-server` modeline on line 1 of `arc.yaml`. Editors were fetching the SchemaStore entry for the architect framework, which matches any file named `arc.yaml`, and reporting the failed download as an error on every open.
- Docs consolidated (2026-09-11): `docs/install.md` is folded into `docs/usage.md` as a collapsed reference section at the end (every install channel, verification, offline notes, the maintainer checklist; sections 1 to 15 keep their numbers so existing cross-references resolve), `docs/data-governance.md` is condensed into a data governance section of `docs/SECURITY.md`, `docs/changelog.md` is `docs/CHANGELOG`, and the README's reference list is collapsed. The hardening plan is maintainer working notes and leaves the tree (gitignored, kept locally); every pointer at it in docs, workflow headers and source comments is reworded. `python/embed_query.py` pointed at a `docs/phi-safety.md` that never existed; it points at the SECURITY.md data governance section.
- CI logs without warnings: the four cargo-fuzz targets are kebab-case (`urna-view`, `section-decoders`, `runtime-indexes`, `mmap-open-search`; source files unchanged), which retires cargo's four name warnings and restarts the nightly soak corpus from the seeds under the new cache keys; `deny.toml` skips `syn@2` in the duplicate-version check with the reason recorded (pyo3-macros 0.29 and zerocopy-derive 0.8 are still on syn 2 while serde, clap, bytemuck and thiserror moved to syn 3), and the forge-core step allows the unmatched skip.

### Added

- Task-utility floor for the `crf = "auto"` gate + the `retrieval-auto` media profile (2026-09-12): `[media.quality]` gains `utility_floor_hit1` (negative = off, the default), `utility_queries`, `utility_query_template` (`{label}`) and `utility_tol`; when enabled, `quality_gate.choose_crf` embeds one text query per sampled item once with the gate model's text tower (`forge_media_stage` threads the row labels; an image-only gate model is a SpecError naming the key) and at every ladder rung measures text-to-image hit@1 against the decoded frames of the stratified sample, passing when `hit1_decoded >= max(utility_floor_hit1, hit1_source - utility_tol)`; the ladder report records `hit1`, `visual_pass`, `drift_pass`, `utility_pass` per rung and `utility.hit1_source` + threshold, and a negative `drift_floor_p10` now disables the drift leg explicitly. Motivation measured on 38627 cards (experiment 13 of brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark): CLIP cosine drift p10 falls 0.932 at crf40 to 0.829 at crf60 and the drift floor vetoes every rung, while text-to-image hit@1 on 100 queries does not move up to crf50; drift is a stability signal, utility is the floor a retrieval profile needs. `retrieval-auto` = intra + still + speed 6 + CRF auto with visual and drift floors disabled, utility floor 0.0, tol 0.02, ladder [40, 45, 50, 55, 60]; `retrieval` keeps its fixed CRF 50. The profile table moves to `python/forge/media_profiles.py` (build_spec was at 297 lines) and a profile's `quality` table now deep-merges with an explicit `[media.quality]` (explicit keys still win); the four new QualitySpec fields enter the media state key, so `--resume` re-encodes an existing gated build once. The utility leg lives in `python/forge/quality_utility.py` (81 lines; quality_gate stays at 217). Tests: `test_quality_gate.py` +3 (pass on a labeled block-colour sample with a stub text+image adapter, utility-only rejection with the report naming the leg, image-only gate model -> SpecError), `test_forge_spec.py` pins the five profiles, the deep merge, the explicit override, and the four range errors.
- `${VAR}` in spec paths + the `retrieval` media profile (2026-09-12): `python/forge/spec_paths.py` expands the braced form only (a bare `$` in SQL or a text template and the `{col}` placeholders survive) over every string of the spec before parsing, an unset or empty variable is a SpecError naming the dotted key (`source.path: ${X} is not set; export X=/path`; before, `corpus_sources._Blank` turned the leftover into a silent `$`, and an empty root would have read `/mtg.sqlite`), then `~/` expands as before; the module resolves SpecError lazily so it imports on its own; `build_spec.py` drops to 292 lines. `forge_manifest.check_lock` now also ignores `source.path`, `source.db` and `source.image.path_template` next to `spec_path` and `output.dir`: the lock stores the expanded strings and a `--rebuild-only --strict-env` under another data root used to fail L3 on the path alone (row content is guarded per item by `input_hash`). `MEDIA_PROFILES["retrieval"]` = GOP intra + tune still + speed 6 + CRF 50, the hand-rolled recipe from the 2026-09-03 full-corpus run (38627 cards: 532671548 B self-contained, 7.46x vs JPEG, no measurable txt@1 loss on 100 queries, drift p10 0.942 under the 0.98 default floor, so the profile pins CRF rather than gating). tests/test_forge_spec.py gains `test_env_expansion` (set -> build of 3 rows validates; unset and empty -> the key and `export` in the message; `$5` survives; `~/` and a variable holding `~/` expand; `models[1].model_path` reaches into lists; a fresh interpreter imports `forge.spec_paths` alone; `--rebuild-only --strict-env` with the variable moved to a copy of the data passes L3) and pins four profile names; 8/8 pass.
- Forge embed cache under a shared content-addressed root (2026-09-12): entries move from `<out>/.cache/<name>.<preset>.npz` (one slot per corpus name and preset) to `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/urna/embed/<preset>/<hash(triad + arrays flags)[:16]>.npz`, SHA256 sidecar and lock beside them, `model_hash` probes under `models/`; override with `[output] cache_dir`, `urna_forge.py --cache-dir`, or `URNA_CACHE_DIR` (spec > flag-set spec > env > XDG), and `--dry-run` prints the resolved root. Motivation measured on the MTG candidates: each of the three output dirs carried an identical 113 MB `.cache` (CLIP 79.1 MB + potion 39.5 MB), and a shared dir with the old name-keyed slot would have thrashed because every MTG spec is named `mtgdataset`. `.forge-state/` and `.tmp/` stay in the output dir (transactional, `os.replace` needs one filesystem); `<name>.media/` stays (the served sidecar). Existing output dirs keep an orphaned `<out>/.cache/` and `<out>/.forge-state/model_hash.<preset>.json` that nothing reads any more; delete them by hand. The `model_hash` probe is keyed by preset plus the knobs that enter the fingerprint (normalize, dtype, device, model_path) and a probe that disagrees with the loaded model is overwritten, so two specs with the same preset and different knobs no longer thrash one probe. The cache location is not identity: `output.cache_dir` is dropped from `build.lock.json` and exempt from the L3 diff, so the same root supplied through the spec, `--cache-dir` (also on the `urna build` launcher) or `URNA_CACHE_DIR` claims L3 under `--strict-env`. An unusable cache root (a file, no permission) is a `SpecError` naming `output.cache_dir` and the three override sources, exit 2 from the CLI. `tests/test_forge_spec.py` pins `URNA_CACHE_DIR` to its tmp dir and proves the user cache is untouched; `test_cache_shared_across_specs` (10 tests now, 10 s): two output dirs with the same rows -> second build `embed.potion` 0.0 and one potion npz whose mtime is untouched, a CRF change adds a second image-space entry (2) instead of overwriting, `cache_dir` in the spec wins over the env var, the same root via `URNA_CACHE_DIR` then passes `rebuild_only` + `strict_env` with `embed.potion` 0.0 and no cache path in the lock, no `<out>/.cache`; `test_corrupt_cache_recomputed` corrupts the one entry its own build wrote and proves the rewrite (mtime moved, sidecar valid again); `test_probe_conflict` plants a wrong potion probe and the build still passes with the probe corrected; `test_cache_root_errors` covers the file-as-root error path.
- HNSW build 1.73x faster on 20k x 384, still single-threaded and byte-deterministic across machines: the build distance is an eight-accumulator fixed-tree dot the compiler vectorizes without FMA on every target, and the build reuses one epoch-stamped visited list instead of allocating a hash set per layer search. The graph bytes are pinned by a test for two seeded builds; the 10k digest moved once, on purpose, with the reason recorded in the test.
- urna-format under Miri: the nightly CI runs `cargo miri test -p urna-format` (tree borrows; tests that need zstd's c code, `half`'s aarch64 inline asm or the fsst table build are marked in the source with the reason). The first run found a stacked-borrows report inside `sha2` 0.11's NEON backend (dependency, tree-borrows clean), and a deliberate `'static` leak in a test helper, now removed.
- HNSW build 1.73x faster on 20k x 384, still single-threaded and byte-deterministic across machines: the build distance is an eight-accumulator fixed-tree dot the compiler vectorizes without FMA on every target, and the build reuses one epoch-stamped visited list instead of allocating a hash set per layer search. The graph bytes are pinned by a test for two seeded builds; the 10k digest moved once, on purpose, with the reason recorded in the test. `docs/benchmarks.md` re-run on the same 100k x 384 rows: Urna hybrid build 224 s -> 173 s (hnswlib 83 s, usearch 109 s), p50 0.94 -> 0.72 ms, cold open 309 -> 292 ms, bytes unchanged.
- `cargo deny` in CI: `deny.toml` checks advisories (yanked = deny), licenses (allowlist = exactly the set in the three lockfiles: MIT, Apache-2.0 incl. the LLVM exception, BSD-2/3, NCSA, Unicode-3.0, Unlicense, Zlib), bans (duplicate versions warn) and sources (crates.io only); the `deny` job runs it on the workspace, forge-core and fuzz on every push and pull request.
- `cargo semver-checks` in CI: the `semver` job checks `urna-format`'s public API on every pull request against the PR's base commit, so a breaking change to the reader API without a version bump fails the PR.
- Nightly fuzz soak: `ci.yml` runs every cargo-fuzz target for 30 minutes on a nightly schedule (or on demand with a chosen duration), carrying the corpus over between nights and uploading crash artifacts; `scripts/fuzz_soak.sh` is the same loop for a local hour-per-target soak after a decoder change.
- Property tests for the section codecs: `crates/urna-format/tests/codec_roundtrip_prop.rs` checks, over generated inputs, that intpack, txt_streams, fsst, zstd_dict and dedup decode to the raw payload byte for byte, that float16/int8/int4 reconstruct within their quantization step and are sized exactly as `expected_embeddings_size` says, and that every encoder is a pure function of its input (two calls, identical bytes). `proptest` is a dev-dependency of urna-format only.
- criterion benches in urna-runtime: `cargo bench -p urna-runtime` runs the SIMD kernels per dtype and dim on the detected backend, the public `search` / `search_ann` path end to end over one 20k-row `.urna` per stored dtype, and the HNSW build at the knobs `docs/benchmarks.md` uses; CI compiles them on every pull request.
- `docs/install.md` (since folded into the reference section of `docs/usage.md`): every install channel (one-liner, Windows, `urna` wheel, Homebrew tap, cargo binstall, Docker, dev build), what each lays down and where, verification (per-file SHA256, Sigstore attestations, the post-release install-test), offline and air-gapped notes, and the maintainer one-time checklist. The file was linked from six places and never existed. The checklist records what the release workflows assume but does not exist yet (verified 2026-09-10): the `hoffresearch/homebrew-urna` tap and its token (the `publish-homebrew-formula` job fails without them), the PyPI trusted publisher and `pypi` environment for `urna`, and the `get.hoffresearch.com` short URL (NXDOMAIN). `v0.3.0` predates the pipeline and carries no artifacts, so the channels serve from the first tag cut after it.
- Dependencies brought to the latest MSRV-1.85-compatible versions in one pass (`cargo update`, 45 packages in the workspace lock incl. clap 4.6.6, bytemuck 1.25.2, PyO3 0.29.2, libc 0.2.189; 11 in forge-core incl. syn 3) and the GitHub actions to their current majors (setup-python v7, upload-artifact v7, download-artifact v8), closing the ground the Dependabot pull requests kept re-opening. The full gate (Rust, Python e2e/builder, forge-core) is green on the new locks.
- CLI regrouped without changing a verb: the engine verbs (inspect, validate, stats, media, search*, benchmark, cite, doctor: file + vector in, never run Python) and the agent verbs (ask, retrieve, build: text or spec in, shell out to the offline embedder / forge) are two groups in `urna --help` (ordered, each summary tagged `[engine]` / `[agent]`, a legend in the footer) and two places in the tree (`cmd/*.rs` vs `cmd/agent/*.rs`). One binary stays: a second one would double the installer, formula and dist matrix for no user gain. The `about` line now says what the tool is.
- `docs/benchmarks.md` + `python/tools/bench_competitors.py` (+ `_bench_systems.py`): Urna exact / hybrid against usearch, hnswlib, sqlite-vec and LanceDB on the same synthetic rows, same queries, same machine: build time, bytes on disk, cold open + first query in a fresh process, warm p50/p99, recall@10 vs brute force, byte-identical rebuild (measured by building twice), built-in integrity check, and the citation claim measured (raw vs zstd text share one content_hash). Every HNSW path at m=16 / ef_construction=200 / ef=100, every build single-threaded, rows drawn from a cluster mixture (i.i.d. Gaussians at 384 dims flatten every HNSW to ~0.3 recall and say nothing). Measured 2026-09-05 on 100k x 384: Urna hybrid recall@10 1.000 at p50 0.94 ms, cold open 310 ms (every checksum verified before the first query), HNSW build 224 s vs hnswlib 92 s / usearch 103 s. The table includes what Urna does not do.
- Docs refreshed against the tree: README (what it is in one plain sentence, CLI in two groups incl. `media`, benchmarks and hardening sections), usage.md (§15 `urna media`, the verb list), llms.txt (verb groups, CI, mutation fuzz, the unsafe/unwrap rules), CONTRIBUTING (ci.yml, deny lints, how to run the harnesses), arc.yaml (2026-09-05 summary, ci / mutation-fuzz / competitor-bench flows, the hardening quality invariant, inventory for every new file, cmd/agent paths).
- Hardening pass (2026-09-05), the reader/runtime contract of docs/SECURITY.md made mechanical. `clippy::unwrap_used` and `clippy::undocumented_unsafe_blocks` are denied workspace-wide (`[workspace.lints]`, tests exempt via clippy.toml); every parse-path `unwrap` on a fixed-width field read went through the new `urna_format::bytes::{le_u32, le_u64, le_f32, array32}` helpers that return `UnexpectedEof`; the six raw-parts casts in `layout/` (header, footer, section entry byte views) and the int8 row view are `bytemuck` casts with compile-checked layouts, so `unsafe` is now only the SIMD kernels and the two `mmap` calls, each with a `// SAFETY:` comment and each kernel with a `# Safety` doc; the safe SIMD dispatchers check lengths with `assert!` in release (they were `debug_assert!`, i.e. absent from the binary that opens third-party files).
- SIMD hot path is allocation-free: the int4 fused kernel took `vec![0.0; dim]` per call on AVX2 and NEON (thousands of malloc/free pairs per query in a rerank), and `row_scales_f32` allocated a second `Vec` per candidate. `dot_f32_i4_blocked` now takes a caller-owned `scratch` row and `Int4EmbeddingsView::row_scales_into` fills a caller-owned buffer; `RerankSource` owns both, allocated once per search, so `score` is `&mut self` and allocation-free on every dtype.
- Mutation-fuzz harnesses on stable, run by `cargo test`: `crates/urna-format/tests/mutation_fuzz.rs` (reader + every section decoder over float32/float16/int8/int4 fixtures with every optional section present) and `crates/urna-runtime/tests/mutation_fuzz.rs` (real mmap open + every search verb). Seeded, so a failure prints its reproducing seed; half the mutations reseal header/section/footer checksums so the corruption reaches the decoders instead of dying at the integrity layer. `URNA_MUTATION_ITERS` scales the run (defaults 1500 / 250; soaked at 62,500 x 4 and 10,000 x 4 clean after the fixes below). `fuzz/` adds four `cargo-fuzz` targets (`urna_view`, `section_decoders`, `runtime_indexes`, `mmap_open_search`) with seeds regenerated by the harnesses (`URNA_FUZZ_SEED_DIR`).
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: fmt, Clippy with the deny lints, build + test on Ubuntu (AVX2) and macOS (NEON), the mutation harnesses at a higher count, the 300-line guard, forge-core's own gate, ruff via the new shared `scripts/ruff_check.sh` (one file list for CI and release_check), and a bounded cargo-fuzz smoke on nightly. Until now no workflow ran `cargo test` on a pull request (release / install-test / pypi only fire on tags and releases).
- `UrnaFile.chunk_ids()` and `UrnaFile.blob_bytes(index)` (urna-python): the chunk identities in file order (== source ordinal order), and single-blob extraction from the inlined 0x17 store. Pulling one asset no longer means exporting the whole store: a multi-GB archive corpus never leaves the file to serve one thumbnail.
- `python/tools/urna_ui_bridge.py`: a JSON bridge so any frontend can drive a corpus without reimplementing engine logic. Three ops: `browse` (paginated manifest items with media resolved to the internal blob URI + shard-local frame; manifest segments are matched to blobs by SHA256, never by name, so renamed provenance can't break resolution), `search` (exact / ann / hybrid / graph and named spaces, every hit enriched with title, media reference and ordinal, matched by `chunk_id`), and `blob` (write ONE inlined blob to disk, atomically). Query embedding routes through the registry with the same `URNA_ALLOW_REMOTE_CODE` opt-in as the query path.
- Measured on a 38,627-image full corpus (2026-09-03), the retrieval-only compression answer: with the visual floors dropped, AV1 all-intra at crf50 serves the whole corpus from a 508 MB self-contained file (7.8x vs the JPEG source) [corrected in the 2026-09-12 note: 533 MB, 7.46x] with NO detectable utility loss against its 3.6 GB lossless twin: same-ruler text→image hit@1 parity and identity@1 = 1.000 (n=100, weak label ruler: "no detectable degradation", not proof of equality). The drift floor would have VETOED that file (CLIP drift p10 0.942), so embedding drift is a stability signal, not a utility floor: a retrieval-only gate needs a hit@k floor, registered as a follow-up. Same battery: AVIF q48 full corpus lands at the crf35 still class's drift with -20% bytes and per-image O(1) access (the I-frame battery's -12.4% prediction, larger at full scale) [corrected in the 2026-09-12 note: -13.0% on the .urna, equal to the prediction], the natural next default for the `stills` profile. The av1-crf40 / av1-crf50 / avif-q48 twins share ONE content_hash: citations survive re-encoding and even a backend swap, media stays outside the canonical sections by design.
- Self-contained media: `[output] embed_media = true` inlines the encoded media bytes into the `.urna` itself via the new `blob_data` section (0x17): an offset table parallel to the `blob_refs` (0x14) record order plus the raw bytes, RAW-encoded, OPTIONAL, and content_hash-excluded like the rest of the blob trio, so the embedded and sidecar twins keep identical citations. The runtime opens the offset table only and slices individual blobs lazily off the mmap (`MmapUrnaFile::blob_bytes`); the new CLI verb `urna media <file> [--export DIR]` lists a corpus's blobs and exports the inlined ones with per-blob SHA256 verification, and `urna validate` now proves every inlined blob against its `blob_refs` content_hash. `urna_forge` builds it through the new `blob_data_paths` kwarg on `urna.build` (shard bytes are read in Rust, never round-tripped through Python objects).
- `embed_query_potion.py --mrl-dim N` (slice + renormalize), and the embed gate now forwards `--mrl-dim` for potion corpora too, so a corpus built with `build.mrl_dim` under the default potion model is queryable instead of failing the dim gate on every `ask`.
- `urna_model_bench.py --images-root DIR`: manifest image paths are provenance-redacted (spec-dir-relative or `~`-prefixed), so the bench now resolves them against an explicit root, then the index dir, then the CWD, and fails with the fixing flag instead of a bare FileNotFoundError.
- `urna benchmark --space NAME` now honors `--madvise-cold`.
- Per-segment GOP (RFC-2): `media.gop = "auto"` on a sharded AV1 build now probes intra-vs-inter once PER SEGMENT instead of once globally, and each segment ships (and records in the manifest) its own keyint. With `order = "cluster"` the near-duplicate runs concentrate in a few segments, measured 2026-08-31: inter with the bounded GOP (keyint=16, scd=0) is -29% on same-artwork reprints, while unique segments keep O(1) all-intra access. Two probe honesty fixes landed with it: (a) under an ENGINEERED order (cluster/similarity) the probe samples contiguous Windows instead of evenly-spaced frames: even spacing was erasing the very adjacency the ordering created, biasing every probe toward intra; the sampling mode is recorded in the probe record; (b) each probe arm now encodes what would actually ship (the intra arm carries the requested tune, the inter arm drops it), so the decision compares real shipping configs.
- `tune = "still"` with an inter GOP no longer hard-errors the encode: SVT's IQ tune is all-intra only, so an inter stream (forced `gop = "inter"`, or an inter-deciding segment under `gop = "auto"`) drops the tune and records `tune_resolved = null`: the requested flag is never silently claimed.
- The GOP probe is quality-aware: at the same CRF SVT quantizes P-frames far coarser, so a bytes-only probe silently trades quality for size (measured 2026-08-31 on unique cards: -10.7% bytes for -15.9 ssimulacra2 p50 and worse CLIP drift). Both probe arms are now scored with ssimulacra2 against the letterboxed sources and inter wins only when its mean is within 2.0 of intra (`PROBE_QUALITY_TOL`); the scores, the tolerance, and any `overridden: inter-degrades-quality` land in the probe record. Soft dependency: without `ssimulacra2` on PATH the probe stays bytes-only and says so in the record and on stderr.
- `probe_tune_still` probes with `keyint=1`: the IQ tune (3 on SVT 4.2) accepts all-intra only, so the old probe rejected it and silently fell back to tune=4 (MS_SSIM), the exact silent-fallback class the probe exists to prevent. Measured on the card corpus: tune=3 is +1.26 ssimulacra2 for +1.4% bytes. Found by the I-frame battery (experiment 11 of the benchmark repository).
- `media.profile`: dataset-type presets (`near-dup`, `stills`, `archive`) resolved into knob defaults before explicit keys, so one line picks the measured recipe for a corpus class without closing off any other use case (an explicit key always wins). Unknown profile is a SpecError listing the valid names.
- `encode_jxl_dir` fans the per-image `cjxl` processes out on a bounded thread pool (min(8, cores)); wall time only, bytes and per-file decisions unchanged; on the 38k-card corpus this is the difference between hours and minutes for the lossless archive build.
- The per-image decode iterator (resume/embed path for JXL/AVIF backends) prefetches on the same bounded pool with a 32-frame in-flight window, order preserved; decoding is deterministic so the embeddings are unchanged; sequential `djxl` over 38k files was the other hours-long stage of the archive build.
- Specs are machine-portable: `load_spec` expands `~/` in every string field, and `resolve_model_dir` expands `~` in a preset's `local_dir`: no more hardcoded home directories in specs or the registry (privacy: a spec or manifest shared outside the machine no longer names the local user).

### Fixed

- AVIF manifest `media.source_bytes` was the sum of the letterboxed PNG intermediates avifenc reads, not the original files (2026-09-12): the 38627-card JPEG corpus recorded 21450566470 bytes and `compression_ratio` 18.61 for a 3975063106-byte source (true ratio 3.45x), and the sample-2048 AVIF rows recorded 1138810355 instead of 211018809. The backend now hands the original file sum into `encode_avif(source_bytes=)`, the PNG sum stays in the record as `letterboxed_input_bytes`, and the ratio is over the originals, so the manifest field is now honest for every backend: AV1, AVIF, JXL and jxl-transcode all report the byte size of the source files. Guarded by two cases in `tests/test_image_corpus.py` (42 cases, all green with avifenc on PATH). Both touched modules were over the line limit and are split along one responsibility each: `image_encode.py` (521 -> 211 lines) sheds `image_encode_still.py` (AVIF + JXL encoders, 194) and `image_gop_probe.py` (the GOP probe, 163); `image_backends.py` (459 -> 239) sheds `image_backends_av1.py` (the stream backend, 216; its keyint decision, `resolve_keyint`, moved next to the probe in `image_gop_probe.py`, 188). No behaviour change beyond the accounting; `test_quality_gate.py` and `test_forge_spec.py` pass unchanged. AVIF corpora built before this fix keep the old record on `--resume` (`.forge-state/media.json` is reused when the input hash and media spec match): re-encode the media stage, or patch `media.source_bytes` and `compression_ratio` in the manifest by hand.
- The stray `l` prefix on 26 forge-core doc comments that the earlier sweep missed (four lowercase-initial: `/// lin the default`, `/// lfield order`, ...), and the `space[0]` rustdoc unresolved-link warning in `embedding_request.rs`. Stale paths: `docs/arc/arc.yaml` named `crates/urna-runtime/src/ann/codec.rs` (split into `codec/mod.rs` + `codec/tests.rs` in #71) and `.github/workflows/dist-build-setup.yml` (moved to `.github/` in 723ba186); `llms.txt` named `data/demo/README.md` (renamed to `Instructions.md` in #99); a urna-format module comment cited the old codec path. arc.yaml counted nine engine subcommands where the CLI has twelve plus three agent verbs, and its inventory lacked `docs/usage.md`, `docs/changelog.md`, `docs/data-governance.md` and `docs/install.md`.
- Fifth finding, from the first coverage-guided run (`cargo fuzz run section_decoders`, 47 million executions of the reader target clean before it): the intpack-repacked spans decoder sized its URI pool from the file's `n_uris` before reading a byte of it, so a 90-byte payload reached `Vec::with_capacity(1.3e9)` and a 31 GB allocation abort. Bounded by `remaining / 4` (every pooled URI costs its length prefix); the two libFuzzer artifacts are pinned in `tests/negative_alloc_claims.rs` and ride in `fuzz/seeds/` as regression seeds.
- Four bug classes found by the first runs of the mutation-fuzz harness, all "malformed file panics the runtime" (in scope per docs/SECURITY.md), each pinned by a regression test: (1) `expected_embeddings_size` multiplied header-controlled `n * dim * width` unchecked (arithmetic-overflow panic in debug, a wrapped product that could match a tiny section in release); now checked, and `Int8/Int4EmbeddingsView::parse` route through it. (2) A NaN lane in a multimodal band (or the fp rerank slab) produced a NaN cosine and `partial_cmp(..).unwrap_or(Equal)` is not a total order, which `sort_by` detects and panics on since Rust 1.81; bands and the fp slab now pass the same finite-values gate as the canonical embeddings at open (`validate_slab_values`), and every runtime sort goes through `order::{cmp_score_desc, cmp_dist_asc}`, NaN-last total orders. (3) The section cursors checked `pos + n > len`, which wraps for an attacker-chosen `n`; every cursor now checks `n > remaining`. (4) BM25 postings carried doc ids never validated against `n_docs` (out-of-bounds index at query time) and non-finite `k1`/`b`/`avgdl` were accepted; both are typed rejections at decode.
- Stale comments: the `layout/mod.rs` section-id map claimed 0x09-0x0C and 0x14-0x17 were "NOT yet emitted or read" while `embeddings_fp`, `dictionary`, `dedup_map`, `graph_adjacency`, `blob_refs`, `space_table`, `blob_span_overlay` and `blob_data` all ship; the comment now states the per-id status. The stray `l` prefix that corrupted 264 `///` doc comments in HEAD (fixed earlier) also sat on 200 `//` and `#` comments across 55 Rust and Python files; all removed.
- `urna_model_bench.py` matched hits by `ordinal == offset_start`, which only holds for single-shard `order = "none"` video corpora: cluster-ordered corpora (permuted frames) and per-image backends (JXL/AVIF, offsets local to each blob) silently benched as 0.000 on every tier. Matching is now by `chunk_id` via `UrnaFile.chunk_ids()`, valid for every backend and ordering, in the label ruler and the `--queries-file` path alike.
- `urna_model_bench.py` expands `${ENV_VAR}` placeholders in manifest image paths (privacy redaction writes them, e.g. a data dir exported as an env name) and fails with the exact `export` line to run when the variable is unset.
- N11 on the query/bench side: `embed_query_model.py` and `urna_model_bench.py` no longer auto-grant `trust_remote_code`/`allow_heavy` from whatever preset an untrusted manifest names; remote-code presets require the explicit `URNA_ALLOW_REMOTE_CODE="<preset>,..."` opt-in (documented in usage §12/§13, SECURITY.md updated, including the corrected `search-space` gate claim: it validates only under `--expect-model-hash`).
- Forge embed-cache key now includes WHICH arrays the spec requests (text/image, and the dedup toggle), and a loaded cache entry missing a required array is recomputed instead of crashing emit with a KeyError; the `model_hash` probe under `.forge-state/` is keyed by a cheap fingerprint of the resolved model dir, so swapping the snapshot on disk invalidates the probe instead of silently emitting the old model's vectors.
- Spec validation: unknown top-level tables are an error (a typo'd `[ouput]` no longer silently no-ops), `[models]` written as a table instead of `[[models]]` is a typed SpecError, and the crf=auto gate model and cluster space are validated independently (each against its own pipeline resolution) instead of through one or-chain that let a typo'd `cluster.space` crash mid-build with a bare StopIteration; `media.order="similarity"` now requires an image model at validate time too.
- `--resume` integrity for per-image backends (JXL/AVIF/control): every frame's file must still exist non-empty, instead of accepting any non-empty media dir and packaging deleted or truncated media.
- A `--models`-filtered build no longer overwrites the full build's `build.lock.json` (a later full `--rebuild-only --strict-env` would have failed on the subset lock); when no lock exists the subset lock records `models_subset`.
- SQLite join misses are visible: a join that matches zero rows is a typed SpecError naming the key (typo or TEXT-vs-INTEGER mismatch), and partial matches print a `hits/total` count instead of silently degrading the canonical text.
- `--dry-run` output list now mirrors emit's per-model skip of the text-default-only duplicate; `--seed` help states plainly that evenly-spaced sampling ignores it today; the stray `l` prefix on 39 clap doc comments no longer corrupts `--help`.

### Added

- Distribution / installation (issue #75). The `urna` CLI now ships through cargo-dist: five release targets (Linux x86_64/aarch64 static musl, macOS x86_64/arm64, Windows x86_64 MSVC) built from tags on `main`, per-artifact `.sha256` plus a combined `sha256.sum`, Sigstore keyless attestations (`gh attestation verify`), a Homebrew formula for the `hoffresearch/homebrew-urna` tap, and cargo-binstall metadata in `crates/urna-cli/Cargo.toml`. The offline embedder payload (potion script + table) rides as its own release artifact, staged by `scripts/stage_embedder_payload.py`. `scripts/install.sh` / `scripts/install.ps1` are the one-liner installers: platform detect, SHA256 verify before any write, binary to `~/.local/bin`, payload to the XDG data dir, `--version` pin and `--uninstall`.
- Python package `urna` on PyPI (issue #75). `packaging/pyproject.toml` + `scripts/stage_wheel.py` stage the maturin project (`packaging/staging/`, gitignored); one `cp312-abi3` wheel per platform (Linux x86_64/aarch64, macOS universal2, Windows amd64) carries `_urna`, the `urna` package, and the bundled potion table (~30 MB) so embedding is offline after install (bundle over lazy fetch, by decision recorded in `docs/install.md`, since folded into the reference section of `docs/usage.md`). `urna.embed_potion` (the `embed` extra: numpy + tokenizers) embeds offline from the installed package. The console entry point `urna` (read-only verbs: validate, inspect, stats, search) makes `uvx --from urna urna ...` work. `python/urna.py` loads `_urna` as a package submodule when installed and falls back to the side-by-side dev `.so`, unchanged for the dev flow. Publishing uses OIDC trusted publishing + PEP 740 attestations (`.github/workflows/pypi.yml`); `.github/workflows/install-test.yml` tests the INSTALLED binary and wheel on every released platform after each release.
- `urna doctor` (issue #75): post-install health check with typed exit codes (0 ok, 2 Python missing, 3 Python deps missing, 4 embedder script missing, 5 potion table missing or git-lfs pointer, 6 embedder run failed). Checks versions, SIMD backend, Python interpreter + deps, potion embedder + table, and runs one real offline embed. The potion embedder resolution now also covers installed layouts: `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/urna/forge/` and `<exe>/../share/urna/forge/`, after the repo layout.
- Integration examples (issue #75): `examples/fastapi`, `examples/flask`, `examples/jupyter` (offline by construction, potion-embedded, cited answers), and `docker/Dockerfile` (static musl binary on `scratch`, corpus via mounted volume, air-gapped friendly, x86_64 for now).

### Fixed

- `scripts/release_check.sh` rebuilds `python/_urna.so` with `--features pyo3/extension-module`. Without it PyO3 emits an explicit libpython link into the cdylib, and under a statically-embedded interpreter (uv's python-build-standalone) importing the extension loads a SECOND Python runtime and segfaults at module init (measured: `test_e2e.py` died at `pthread_mutex_lock` on this machine). Extension modules must resolve Python symbols from the host process; maturin builds the published wheel the same way, so the dev flow now matches it. The README dev-build instructions apply unchanged for framework Pythons, where the old link happened to work.
- fsst symbol-table build no longer stalls large builds. The frequency pass counted every 1-8 byte substring through a `BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, u64>`, paying a `to_vec` allocation and an ordered-tree insert per window (~54 s for 5k chunks, superlinear; a 30k-chunk preset build effectively never finished). Counting now uses a plain `HashMap` (the ranking comparator was already explicitly deterministic, so iteration order never fed the output) and the greedy longest-match encoder walks a byte trie instead of scanning all 255 symbols per position. Same corpus, same chosen table, byte-identical output: the `compressed` ladder rung still measures 0.339x with matching hashes. Measured on the 30,725-chunk corpus: the fsst candidate evaluation went from effectively-unbounded to ~41 s. `crates/urna-format/src/encoding/fsst.rs` also split the table/trie code into `fsst_table.rs` to stay under the 300-line source limit.

### Changed

- Contributor docs describe the branch model actually in use: `main` is the only long-lived branch, work happens on short-lived branches off it, and every change lands as a squash-merged pull request under the `main` ruleset (pull request required, verified SSH-signed commits, linear history); delete the branch after merge. `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md` and `llms.txt` previously told contributors to branch from and target a `dev` integration branch that does not exist on the remote. The installer headers (`scripts/install.sh`, `scripts/install.ps1`) show the raw GitHub one-liner the README uses instead of the unregistered short URL, and the README install section says that `v0.3.0` predates the release pipeline.
- The offline embedder now resolves its Python interpreter in a fixed order instead of a single hard-coded `python3`: an explicit `URNA_PYTHON` wins, else the repo's `.venv/bin/python` discovered by walking up to four ancestors of the CWD, else `python3` on PATH. `urna search-text` previously always spawned `python3` and `ask`/`retrieve` only honored `URNA_PYTHON`, so on a machine whose system `python3` lacks numpy + tokenizers (the potion-table deps) the flagship verbs failed until the user set `URNA_PYTHON` or fixed PATH by hand; the repo `.venv` is now picked up with no extra setup. The embedder opens no socket regardless of interpreter, so the offline-by-construction guarantee is unchanged. Centralized in `urna-cli`'s `cmd::pyenv::resolve_interpreter`, with a `resolve_interpreter_from` core unit-tested for the override / `.venv`-discovery / `python3`-fallback precedence. The resolved interpreter is logged to stderr (the choice is never silent), and the ancestor-search trust assumption is documented: with `URNA_PYTHON` unset, discovery executes the nearest ancestor `.venv/bin/python`, so set `URNA_PYTHON` explicitly when running from an untrusted tree.

### Added

Image and PDF corpus builder (experimental, forge tooling layer only, no format or runtime change):

- `python/forge/embed_image.py` is a vision embedder over `open_clip` (DermLIP for dermatology, a plain architecture plus a pretrained tag for other domains). `model_hash` fingerprints the weights that were actually loaded plus the preprocess transform, not the model name, so the manifest gate can tell two checkpoints apart. A bare architecture name without a pretrained tag is now rejected: open_clip answers that request with RANDOM weights, and a corpus built on random weights searches like noise while looking healthy.
- `python/forge/image_media.py` owns the codec path. Every image is letterboxed onto one canvas derived from the dataset's median aspect ratio and median width, with `--width` acting as a ceiling rather than a target so a corpus is never upscaled into encoding interpolated pixels. Frames move through a rawvideo pipe rather than the concat demuxer, which re-derives timestamps per input and drops frames it reads as out of order. The encoded frame count is verified against the item count, because a corpus whose `#frame=N` pointers are off by one is worse than no corpus.
- `python/forge/image_items.py` is the discovery layer. An item's position becomes its corpus ordinal, its frame number, and the `byte_start` its citation resolves through, so the ordering is sorted and reproducible, and sampling renumbers densely.
- `python/tools/urna_build_image_corpus.py` emits the `.urna`, one chunk per image or PDF page. Precomputed embeddings are handed to `Pipeline` keyed by `chunk_id`, never by position: `Pipeline` passes only the chunks the scratch cache missed, so a positional lookup silently assigns other images' vectors on any partially warm rebuild.
- A corpus is `corpus.urna` beside `corpus.media/`, with `media://<file>#frame=N` URIs relative to that pair, so it can be copied to another machine and still resolve. `corpus.manifest.json` records ordinals, origins, labels, PDF page numbers, and the media SHA256.
- `python/tools/urna_search_image.py` queries through `retrieve` (so the `model_hash` gate runs), resolves hits back to their original file and page, and can decode the matched frames out of the corpus media.
- `python/tools/urna_image_eval.py` replaces the earlier recall script and reports two rulers separately, never blended. Every delta against the control carries a paired percentile bootstrap (5000 resamples, seeded) and a `significant` flag, so a difference the sample cannot resolve is not reported as a finding.
- PDF pages carry their page number into the manifest and into the citable text, and the eval harness re-renders a page from its source PDF when it needs the original pixels, since the build-time renders are temporaries. Without that a PDF corpus could be built but never measured.
- `tests/test_image_corpus.py` builds its dataset in-test instead of pointing at a local disk path, and covers frame alignment, the partly-warm cache path, relocatability, the model_hash gate, PDF page provenance, seeded sampling with dense renumbering, letterbox geometry, the no-upscale canvas ceiling, and the bootstrap's refusal to call a sub-resolution difference significant. The vision model is not needed to run it, and `release_check.sh` now runs it.

### Added

Declarative builds, pluggable model registry, dataset compression levers, space-aware CLI (RFCs 0-5, no format change; everything rides the existing 0x14/0x15/0x16/0x20+ sections):

- Model registry (`python/forge/model_registry.py`): named presets; `potion`, `clip-vit-b32`, `siglip2` (open_clip ViT-B-16-SigLIP2/webli), `jina-v5-omni-nano`/`-small`, `wemm-2b`/`-4b`/`-9b` (Tencent wemm-Embedding, sentence-transformers, trust_remote_code), `fake-test` (env-gated, deterministic, no ML; the test double). A preset declares ids, the VALIDATED MRL ladder (`mrl={supported,dims,method="prefix_slice_l2"}`; dims outside the model card's ladder are refused, slicing at 300 just because it is mathematically possible would be false advertising), modalities, deps with the exact pip fix line, and the asymmetric usage contract (`text_corpus_mode`/`text_query_mode`/`image_prompt`/`preprocess_version`/`image_max_side`); spec-overridable, all recipe-hashed. Remote-code presets load only with an explicit `output.allow_remote_code=["<preset>"]` opt-in AND matching pinned code-file hashes (`verify_remote_code`); a hash identifies a version, it does not make it safe, so the opt-in is the consent and the pin is the identification. `wemm-4b`/`-9b` are registered but `executable=False`: `--allow-heavy` or refusal. `slice_renorm` mirrors the engine's MRL truncate-then-renormalize (equivalence proven against `urna.build(mrl_dim=)` in `python/forge/test_model_registry.py`).
- st backend (`python/forge/embed_st.py`): wemm/Jina via sentence-transformers with `encode_query`/`encode_document` routing, per-preset `encode_kwargs` (Jina needs `task="retrieval"`; presets are data, so that fix is a table entry), `model_hash` = fingerprint convention over weights/tokenizer/processor + SHA256 of every remote-code file + pooling/normalize/dtype policy. dtype default measured on this machine: fp16 on MPS (wemm-2b image embeds 0.5s vs 23s in fp32), bf16 on CUDA, fp32 on CPU; `image_max_side` (wemm default 768) bounds the vision-tower patch count and is part of the embedding recipe. `image_doc_format` is a per-preset contract field the three-tier bench caught the hard way: handing Jina image documents as `{"image", "text": prompt}` dicts (wemm's contract) collapses its image space onto the shared prompt text; cosines 0.99 between UNRELATED cards, identity recall 0.000; while the bare PIL form discriminates (0.40-0.46); wemm keeps "dict", Jina gets "bare", both recipe-hashed so the triad invalidates exactly the affected cache. Adapters for st models run in one persistent WORKER PROCESS each (`python/forge/embed_st_worker.py`): loading two trust_remote_code st models in one process collides in transformers' dynamic-module machinery (measured: Jina's custom_st wrapper ended up calling wemm's weights and every image forward raised; purging sys.modules was not enough), and the worker also returns the model's memory when the adapter closes.
- Declarative builds (`urna build --spec corpus.toml`, launcher over `python/tools/urna_forge.py` → `forge/build_spec.py` + `forge/corpus_sources.py` + `forge/forge_pipeline.py`): one TOML describes source (sqlite query + joins + derive + text template with drop-empty-line rendering | csv | jsonl | image_dir), media, models and outputs; unknown keys are SpecErrors naming the key, `order_by` must be a TOTAL order (uniqueness of the composite key verified against the loaded rows), exactly one `text="default"` model is space 0 of EVERY emitted file (no implicit injection). Output modes `single` | `per-model` | `both` share one media encode and one embed pass per model; chunk_ids are content-addressed so citations are identical across output modes (tested). Identity model: three hashes, never conflated; `model_hash` (the model), per-item `input_hash` (canonical text ⊕ source image bytes ⊕ label ⊕ chunker), `embedding_recipe_hash` (prompts, modes, preprocess, dtype, device class, decoder fingerprint when embedding decoded media); the embed cache key is the triad, so a retranslated text or re-exported image invalidates exactly what it should. Builds are transactional (per-stage state under `.forge-state/`, tmp + atomic rename, `--resume`), caches are flock'd with checksum sidecars (torn write ⇒ recompute, never reuse), every build emits a versioned manifest (`manifest_schema_version=1`, canonical serialization, provenance modes `minimal|standard|full` with path/SQL redaction) and a `build.lock.json` pinning package versions, tool binaries (SHA256), model hashes and the materialized spec; byte-identical (`--rebuild-only`) is only claimable under a matching lock (`--strict-env` turns divergence into an error). Reproduction levels are declared: L1 same top-k anywhere, L2 vector-tolerant same device class, L3 byte-identical under the lock.
- Dedup: identical source images (by content hash, computed once and shared with the input_hash) enter the stream once; all duplicate rows point at the same frame through the 0x16 span overlay; n chunks to one frame with zero format change. Measured potential on the full Scryfall printings set: 100,452 printings share 51,870 unique arts (~48% of media avoidable).
- Media levers: `tune="still"` (SVT-AV1 still-picture tune, PROBED against the local encoder; an unsupported value becomes a recorded fallback and a stderr warning, never a silently ignored flag), `speed`, `fps` (timestamps only; verified frames are 1:1 with items), and `crf="auto"`; a dual quality gate (`python/forge/quality_gate.py`): stratified ssimulacra2 floors (deterministic versioned bucket heuristics: resolution/entropy/has_text/alpha/source_format; per-bucket p10 + global min, a global average would let one whole stratum degrade) AND an embedding-drift floor (cosine of source-embed vs decoded-embed by a declared gate model; an image can look fine to humans and still move in retrieval space). The largest ladder CRF clearing both floors wins; none ⇒ smallest + warning; the whole ladder report lands in provenance and the manifest. On the mtgdataset 1500 verification build the gate correctly REFUSED the entire [30,45] ladder at the default floors (fine card text at 488x680 does not reach ssimulacra2 p10>=85) and fell back loudly; the honest result.
- JXL backend (`jxl` = lossless of the source pixels via `cjxl -d 0`; `jxl-transcode` = reversible JPEG repack via `--lossless_jpeg=1`, round-trip VERIFIED by reconstructing with djxl and comparing SHA256). Non-transcodable inputs follow `on_unsupported_jpeg = error | copy-source | lossless-jxl` with the per-file decision recorded. Preservation contract stated, not implied: decoded pixels (JXL), original JPEG bytes (verified transcode); EXIF/ICC/XMP only via `keep_metadata`; timestamps and filenames live in the manifest only. The only truly lossless modes in the media cardápio.
- Cluster ordering (`image_order.cluster_order`): greedy cosine-threshold clustering with deterministic tie-breaks (first-occurrence argmax, ordinal-ordered members, size-desc concat) so near-duplicates become adjacent for per-segment inter prediction; O(n·k·d), capped and documented; ANN-based neighbourhoods are a registered evolution.
- Space-aware CLI: `urna search-space <file> <qvec> --space NAME -k K [--expect-model-hash H]` (typed errors from the runtime, never a silent text-path fallback), `stats` prints a spaces block, `inspect --json` gains `spaces[] {name, space_index, dim, dtype, model_hash, n_vectors, band_bytes}` (`crates/urna-runtime/src/inspect.rs`), `benchmark --space NAME` benches the named band at its own dim. The clap surface moved to `crates/urna-cli/src/cli.rs` (main.rs keeps dispatch; both under the 300-line guard).
- Query routing (`crates/urna-cli/src/cmd/embed_gate.rs`): the spawn protocol + three-layer gate (name → dim → model_hash) now live in ONE module (they were duplicated across `search_text.rs` and `util.rs` and could drift). `ask`/`retrieve`/`search-text` route by the manifest's `embedding_model`: potion corpora keep the potion script byte-for-byte; any registry model goes through the new `python/forge/embed_query_model.py` (same argv/stdout contract, `--preset`, `--mrl-dim` for truncated default spaces, query-role encoding for asymmetric models). A corpus whose default text space is wemm or CLIP is now askable with no new flag.
- Three-tier model bench (`python/tools/urna_model_bench.py`), tiers measured and reported separately, never aggregated: T1 pipeline stability (identity self-retrieval with fresh source-image queries; inflated by construction and labeled as such; plus drift), T2 codec cost (per-item drift; the full two-index source-vs-decoded retrieval delta belongs to the sweep), T3 task utility (label-template text→image, declared weak ground truth, and `--queries-file` operator queries with expected/negative ids: hit@k, MRR, negative leakage). `urna_image_sweep.py` gains `av1-tune`, `jxl`, `jxl-transcode` variants.
- Verification numbers (mtgdataset 1500-card sample, one 4.3 MB multi-space .urna + 81 MB AV1 media, 60 seeded queries, ruler = "artwork of the card {name}" against the model's own image space; weak ground truth, declared): T1 identity@1 = 1.000 for all four image models (the codec preserves each model's own signal); T2 drift p10 = 0.967 (CLIP) / 0.982 (SigLIP2) / 0.989 (jina@256) / 0.990 (wemm-2b@256); T3 text→image hit@1 = 0.233 (CLIP) / 0.850 (SigLIP2) / 0.617 (jina@256) / 0.933 (wemm-2b@256); the models that read the printed card name find the exact card by name, CLIP sees only the art, and wemm holds 93% even sliced to 256 of 2048 dims. Embed cost on this machine (MPS fp16): CLIP ~17 it/s, SigLIP2 ~10 it/s, wemm-2b ~0.6 it/s at image_max_side=768, jina-nano ~0.3 it/s at native resolution (an image_max_side default for Jina is an open follow-up; it changes the recipe, so it invalidates that cache by design).
- File hygiene and doc layout: the new forge modules honor the 333-line contract by splitting along responsibility seams (`model_registry.py` keeps the preset table and gates, `model_adapters.py` the four adapter classes; `forge_pipeline.py` keeps the stages, `forge_emit.py` the blob tables/space payloads/emit/finalize; `build_spec.py` keeps the dataclass contract and parser, `spec_rules.py` the validation, re-exported so importers are unchanged). `data/demo/README.md` was renamed to `data/demo/Instructions.md`; the full worked build-spec example lives in usage §13, so docs reference the contract, never a machine-local corpus.
- Compatibility matrix, tested not presumed: the v0.3.0 reader validates and searches (space 0) a new multi-space+blobs file; additive sections hold. The v0.2.0 reader fails on ANY current file with `unsupported section encoding: section=1 encoding=4`; that break is the text-codec repack (intpack) shipped between v0.2.0 and v0.3.0, pre-existing and now stated instead of implied by "format v1 unchanged".
- Tests wired into `scripts/release_check.sh` (plus RUFF_TARGETS): `tests/test_forge_spec.py` (spec errors, fake e2e, triad invalidation, dedup, output modes, L3 rebuild, torn-cache recompute), `tests/test_quality_gate.py` (both gate legs reject independently; JXL round-trip; cluster determinism), `tests/test_cli_space.py` (8 cases against the release binary), `tests/test_query_embedder_routing.py` (routing observable, `--mrl-dim`, slice≡engine), `python/forge/test_model_registry.py`, `python/forge/test_embed_st.py`.

### Note (2026-08-05): the release gate was red before this change, and one stage still is

`release_check.sh` did not run to completion on `main`. It died at the 300-line guard on `crates/urna-runtime/src/ann/codec.rs` (314 lines), so every stage after it was unreachable. Splitting that file made the rest of the gate reachable and surfaced two more pre-existing failures: `python/tools/measure_presets.py` failed `ruff format --check` (fixed here, whitespace only, the emitted JSON is unchanged), and `exact.p95_ms` fails its latency ceiling.


### Note (2026-08-05): image corpus ruler provenance

The first draft of this feature reported `recall@10 = 1.00` at ~20x compression on PH2. That number was measured on a self-retrieval ruler: the query image is the source of the very frame being looked for, so the corpus contains the answer and the score mostly reports that the codec did not destroy it. Run on the UNCOMPRESSED control the same ruler returns exactly 1.000 at every k, which is what it is worth. It is the same ruler class already flagged for the text ladder above.

Measured numbers, PH2 dermoscopy (n=200, 3 classes, DermLIP ViT-B-16, AV1 CRF 35, canvas 766x576, 1 fps), all 200 images as queries embedded from the ORIGINAL pixels:

- Media 74.70 MB of source JPEG to 1.89 MB, 39.5x. The whole shippable corpus, index included, is 2.10 MB, 35.5x.
- Identity ruler (self-retrieval, inflated by construction): compressed `recall@1` 0.860, `@5` 0.985, `@10` 0.995, against an uncompressed control that is 1.000 everywhere.
- Label ruler (the query's own frame excluded, score is the share of remaining neighbours sharing its diagnosis): compressed `precision@1` 0.695, `@10` 0.576; uncompressed control `precision@1` 0.730, `@10` 0.611; random-pick baseline for this label distribution 0.357.

The finding is the delta, not either column. At 39.5x, AV1 costs 3.5 points of label `precision@10`, bootstrap 95 percent CI [-6.1, -1.1] over 5000 paired resamples of the 200 queries, so the cost is real and not a sampling artifact. What remains is still far clear of the random baseline. Single dataset, single codec setting, no claim beyond that.

### Note (2026-08-07): two corrections to the numbers published two days ago

Both were found by controls that should have been run before publishing, and both change what the earlier note claimed.

**The ratio was understated, because the pipeline was upscaling.** `canvas_size` treated `--width` as a target rather than a ceiling, so the 1024 default resized a 765-wide source UP by 34 percent and paid AV1 to encode interpolated pixels. `--width` is now a ceiling clamped to the median source width, and nothing else changed:

| Canvas | Media | Ratio | Single-frame decode | Label `precision@10` |
| ------ | ----- | ----- | ------------------- | -------------------- |
| 1024x768 (upscaled) | 2.89 MB | 25.9x | 124 ms | 0.593 |
| 766x576 (source) | 1.89 MB | 39.5x | 79 ms | 0.576 |

35 percent off the file and 36 percent off random access. The `precision@10` difference between those two rows is 1.7 points with a 95 percent CI of [-3.8, +0.4], so at n=200 it is not distinguishable from noise: the smaller file is not measurably paid for.

**The earlier "AV1 costs 1.9 points of `precision@10`" was not a supported claim.** That delta's CI is [-4.2, +0.5] and crosses zero. At 25.9x the codec's cost was simply below what 200 queries can resolve, and reporting a point estimate without an interval implied a precision the experiment did not have. The cost only becomes measurable once the ratio goes past roughly 26x:

| Setting | Ratio | Label `precision@10` | Delta vs uncompressed control | Verdict |
| ------- | ----- | -------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------- |
| Uncompressed control | 1.0x | 0.611 | - | - |
| 1024 canvas, GOP 161 | 25.9x | 0.593 | -1.8 pts, CI [-4.2, +0.5] | Within noise |
| Source canvas, GOP 161 | 39.5x | 0.576 | -3.5 pts, CI [-6.1, -1.1] | Real |
| Source canvas, all-intra | 44.7x | 0.570 | -4.1 pts, CI [-7.0, -1.1] | Real |

`--all-intra` (`keyint=1`) stays a lever, default off. It follows from the upstream finding that reordering images changes the ratio by only ~6 percent, so the gain is intra-frame: unrelated images give motion estimation nothing to find and the bits it spends searching are wasted. It buys another 5.2x for 0.6 points of `precision@10`, a difference well inside the interval, and every frame becomes a keyframe so random access has nothing to seek back to.

For the codec-vs-codec question, at matched source resolution and measured on the same 200 images: source JPEG 74.70 MB (6.8 bits/pixel, stored near-lossless), a fair JPEG q95 re-encode 31.87 MB, WebP q90 19.44 MB, JPEG q85 16.20 MB, AV1 crf35 1.94 MB. So AV1 is ~16x smaller than a fair JPEG q95 baseline, and roughly 2.3x of the headline 39.5x is the source having been stored wastefully rather than anything the codec did.

### Changed (2026-08-10, supersedes one line of the 2026-08-07 note)

- The GOP is no longer a flag the caller must guess. `gop_policy="auto"` is the builder default: an evenly spaced sample (32 frames) is probe-encoded both ways at the target CRF and the smaller stream wins, because fase 0 measured that embedding cosine does not separate intra-favouring from inter-favouring corpora, so the policy is a measured encode decision. The probe statistics (n_samples, intra_bytes, inter_bytes, decision) land in the manifest next to the toolchain provenance. `--gop-policy intra|inter` forces a side, and `--all-intra` keeps working as the explicit intra override. The 2026-08-07 note's "lever, default off" described the flag era.
- `build_corpus` moved from `python/tools/urna_build_image_corpus.py` to `python/forge/image_corpus.py` (the tool is a thin CLI re-exporting it), keeping every first-party module under 300 lines.

### Added (2026-08-10): media blob sections, multimodal space bands, scale levers (all additive within frozen format v1, all excluded from content_hash, so a corpus with any of them keeps the citations of its text-only twin)

- `0x14 blob_refs` + `0x16 blob_span_overlay` (F4b), the self-contained single-file layout for media corpora. The codecs raise typed errors on every truncation with hostile-count bounds before any allocation. Both join `OPTIONAL_SECTIONS` behind the new additive `capabilities_ext.blobs_present` flag. The overlay rewrites blob-pointing spans in place at open, so `cite` and `retrieve` report real blob-relative byte ranges instead of the 0x03 ordinal placeholders; a dangling blob_ref_index fails open with a typed error. `urna.build(blob_refs=..., chunk_blob_spans=...)`, `UrnaFile.has_blobs`, `UrnaFile.blob_refs()`, and `inspect` lists the blob table.
- `0x15 space_table` + per-space vector bands `0x20-0x2F` (fp sources `0x30-0x3F`) (F4c): text and vision vectors in the same frozen file, each space with its own `model_hash` and dim, behind `capabilities_ext.supports_multimodal`. Bands follow the embeddings encoding rule (dtype encodings only, never zstd) and the reader validates every listed band against its (n_vectors, dim, dtype). `search_space(name, query, k, expected_model_hash)` scores the band with the shared exact-cosine kernel; the text paths never read a band and the space path never reads 0x04, so a text query can never be scored against vision by accident (isolation test). `urna.build(spaces=[(name, model_hash, dtype, vectors)])`, `UrnaFile.search_space` / `has_spaces` / `space_names`.
- Fase 2 pipeline hardening (forge tooling layer): seek-based random access (`decode_frame`, `decode_frames_at`: one FFmpeg invocation resolves k ordinals; measured 42-67 ms against the legacy select scan's 97-189 ms, byte-identical to the sequential path, asserted on both GOP and all-intra media); toolchain provenance (`toolchain` + `provenance_sha256` on every encode, because another encoder version produces other pixels and an index that silently stops matching the media); per-frame `sha256` recorded in the manifest with `verify_frame_hashes`, which catches a REORDERED stream the frame-count guard cannot see; `pix_fmt` probed after every encode (this libsvtav1 converts 444 to 420 silently, and the lie now raises); symmetric letterbox preprocessing on queries (`--letterbox-query`); text queries through the CLIP text tower (`--query-text`); the AVIF backend (per-image O(1) semantics and real yuv444, the chroma the measured melanoma breakdown asks for on medical corpora); the letterbox-lossless control build mode (`--control`), the ruler every codec cost is measured against.
- Fase 3 measurement harness: `python/tools/_image_metrics.py` (paired bootstrap delta with ci95, sign test, per-class floor, cosine drift, Kendall tau-b plus overlap) and `python/tools/urna_image_sweep.py` (one model load, control first, the whole variant matrix in-process, every delta against the SAME control with an interval, a sign test, and the per-class floor; the mean is not the gate, the worst class is). The control now records its own byte size and every variant reports `urna_bytes`, because the per-backend `compression_ratio` was never the same unit (AV1 sums original files, AVIF sums its letterboxed PNG inputs) and the dtype ladder only moves the index.
- Fase 5 scale levers (forge tooling layer): the GOP probe above; `--shard-size` splits the AV1 stream into consecutive ~1000-frame segments with a segment index in the manifest (URIs name segment plus local frame, so the read side resolves unchanged; true append with index merge is NOT implemented and is declared as such); `--order-similarity` permutes frames by a greedy nearest-neighbour walk before encode, with URIs, vectors, and frame hashes un-permuted back to item order (the tests decode every URI and compare content hashes to prove it); `--dtype` overrides the preset's vector dtype, and the sweep gains `dtype:f32/f16/int8/int4` variants that ride on the preset base to isolate quantization, plus an `av1-order` kind that pins inter (ordering is byte-invisible under all-intra). Caveat recorded: DermLIP is not MRL-trained, so prefix truncation should degrade more than on an MRL model; PCA is the documented fallback if int4/int8 collapse.

### Note (2026-08-10): the full matrix, measured with intervals (fase 6)

Every number below comes from the shipped sweep (`python/tools/urna_image_sweep.py`) against the letterbox-lossless control, and every delta carries a paired bootstrap ci95; JSON artifacts under `tmp/fase6/` (not tracked). "vs control" sizes use the control's own media bytes, the only ruler the variants share.

- PH2 dermoscopy (n=200, DermLIP ViT-B-16, canvas 766x576, 100 queries, control media 144.0 MB, control label precision@10 0.621, random baseline 0.357): AV1 intra crf35 1.67 MB (86x vs control), crf40 1.20 MB (120x); mean precision@10 deltas -3.4 to -4.7 pts with CIs that mostly cross zero at this n. The class floor does not cross: melanoma -16.9 pts CI [-25.0, -10.0] at crf35 yuv420, significant, while both nevus classes are flat. avif444 at the same rate mitigates to -12.5 [-18.8, -6.3]; vector quantization over the same decoded frames is not the driver (melanoma -21.3 on f16, -21.3 on int8, -24.4 on int4, overlapping intervals). The mean said "about -3.5 pts"; the floor says a diagnostic class pays six times that.
- HAM10000 (sample 2000 seed 42 of 10015, 200 queries, canvas 600x450, control media 801.0 MB): AV1 intra crf35 5.31 MB (0.66 percent of the control, 151x). Mean label precision@10 delta -1.5 pts CI [-3.5, +0.6], crossing zero. Per class: nv +1.9 (n=149, not significant), mel -10.7 CI [-15.7, -6.4] (n=14, significant), bkl -13.3 CI [-24.4, -2.2] (significant), vasc -40.0 (n=3, floor trip). int8 quantization repeats the same deltas to the first decimal. The per-class n is small by construction of the sample; the intervals are the honest width of that.
- Text-tower ruler (6 clinical queries through DermLIP's text tower, target-label hits in top-10, base-rate expectation ~6.3/60): control 44/60, crf35 22/60, crf30 26/60. Text queries beat the base rate at every rate point (3.5x at crf35), but the codec cost on the text ruler dwarfs the image ruler's and is NOT recovered by spending 56 percent more bytes at CRF 30: lossy pixel perturbation shuffles tight cross-modal margins instead of being rate-limited. A corpus whose primary use is clinical text search should budget for that gap or stay lossless; this is now a documented limitation with numbers, not an impression.
- WSI tiles (n=1210 in scan order, canvas 512x512, control media 612.9 MB): intra 31.9 MB (5.2 percent), inter 34.4 MB (5.6 percent); the probe picks intra. Similarity ordering on the inter stream: -0.15 percent (slightly worse). The fase 0 +6.6 percent ordering figure does not transfer even to scan-ordered material, so the flag stays opt-in and the novelty claim below drops "ordering" entirely.
- PDF pages (508 pages of a 1907 public-domain bird book, canvas 1024x1464, control media 166.2 MB): intra 37.1 MB (22.3 percent), drift median 0.983. Same codec, same CRF, and the ratio collapses from 151x (dermoscopy) to 19x (tiles) to 4.5x (book pages): the compression ratio is a property of the domain, not of the codec.
- The GOP probe's decisions on real corpora agree with fase 0: intra won on PH2, HAM10000, WSI tiles, and PDF pages, and the probe said so on bytes in every build above.

### Note (2026-08-10): prior art, verified against primary sources, and the novelty claim with its refutations

No citation from memory; every reference below was re-verified against its primary source on 2026-08-10.

What is NOT new, and who did it first: storing an image collection as a video stream is LeRobot's format for robot demonstrations (MP4 for visual observations, Parquet for tabular; Cadene et al. 2024, described e.g. in arXiv 2503.14734 appendix D) and xarrayvideo's for Earth-system cubes (Pellicer-Valero, Aybar, Camps-Valls, "Video compression for spatiotemporal Earth system data", arXiv 2506.19656, reporting 20-50x). Per-image AV1-in-HEIF is AVIF (AOMedia AV1-AVIF spec v1.1.0; Han et al., "A technical overview of AV1", arXiv 2008.06091). The neighbouring codec sciences are JPEG XL (ISO/IEC 18181; Sneyers et al., arXiv 2506.05987) and the neural codecs DCVC (Li, Li, Lu, NeurIPS 2021, arXiv 2109.15047) and ELIC (He et al., CVPR 2022, pp. 5718-5727). Vector search at scale is FAISS (Douze et al., arXiv 2401.08281). Lossy compression in clinical pipelines is governed by the DAIC line (ACR-AAPM-SIIM technical standard for electronic practice of medical imaging, 2017 revision; the ESR position paper on irreversible compression, Insights into Imaging 2011), which is exactly why the per-class floor exists: "diagnostically acceptable" is a per-task property, and a mean cannot show it.

Refutations, attached so the claim cannot grow later: (1) ordering frames by visual similarity does not multiply compression (+6.6 percent on unrelated images in fase 0, -0.15 percent on scan-ordered WSI tiles in fase 6). (2) AV1 over EMBEDDINGS is catastrophic, not clever: recall@1 97 percent to 5.9 (fase 0), so the vectors are embedded from decoded frames, never compressed themselves. (3) The headline ratio is a property of the domain, not the codec: 4.5x to 151x at identical settings across the three fase 6 domains. (4) The text-tower gap above: codec-backed corpora answer text queries well above chance but measurably worse than their lossless control at CRF 30-35.

The claim that survives verification: Urna is, to our knowledge and checked against the sources above, the only system that combines a codec-backed corpus with citation-grade provenance (four hashes, per-frame content hashes, toolchain fingerprint), a per-space model_hash gate with hard text/vision isolation inside one frozen single-file format, two-ruler measurement where every published delta carries an interval and a per-class floor, and a build-time policy probe that decides the GOP on measured bytes. Each element exists elsewhere; the combination inside one hash-verified file does not, per the verified sources.

### Test surface (2026-08-10)

- 340 Rust tests in the sovereign workspace (`cargo test --release --workspace`; was 288 in v0.3.0), including the blob_refs and space_table roundtrips, their negative fuzz suites, reserved_ids band disjointness, the runtime blob overlay and space isolation cases, and the content_hash-equality assertions that keep citations stable.
- Python: `tests/test_image_corpus.py` at 37 cases (was 15 in v0.3.0), plus `tests/test_blob_bridge.py` (5) and `tests/test_space_bridge.py` (5), all run by `release_check.sh`; the full gate is green on this change.

### Note (2026-09-12): the card corpus benchmark, hypotheses and verdicts

The MTG card corpus benchmark (38,627 Magic card scans compressed and indexed into single-file `.urna` containers, the corpus behind the 2026-09-03 numbers in the "added" section above) now lives outside this repository: the code, specs, corpora as id lists, one `results.json` per experiment and the docs are in the `brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark` repository on GitHub, and the `.urna` artifacts are on the Hugging Face dataset `brennercruvinel/mtg-urna-benchmark`; both are private for now. The `RESULTS.md` there is generated by `benchmark/tools/render_report.py` from the per-experiment `results.json` files and is never edited by hand (`--check` is the CI gate). Every number with an artifact on disk was audited against the files and manifests on 2026-09-12; rows 1, 4, 9 and 13 of the table and the txt@k figures behind row 11 are transcribed from the 2026-09-03 report, their artifacts were not retained. Where the audit disagrees with a line above, the audit wins, and the corrections are listed after the table.

| # | Hypothesis | Origin | How tested | Verdict |
| ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | AV1 video compresses better than loose JPEG | Premise of the v0.2 build | Full build of 38,627 cards | Confirmed, with loss: 3.34x on the normal class, 4.45x on art_crop |
| 2 | The stream duplicates frames, each card sits about 1 s on screen | User | ffprobe count_frames on the v0.2 stream | Refuted: one frame per card, fps=1 is only a timestamp |
| 3 | Speeding the video up or lowering fps halves the bytes (the CapCut observation) | User | fps30 intra vs still-s6; programmatic reproduction of the CapCut export | Refuted: fps alone is byte-identical; CapCut's -57% comes from dropping 993 of 2048 cards and re-encoding H.264 |
| 4 | More visual similarity, more compression through inter prediction | User | Corpus B, 2787 same-artwork reprints via illustration_id, GOP 8/16/32, grouped vs shuffled | Confirmed: -29% with inter GOP 16 grouped vs intra, bytes at fixed CRF |
| 5 | Inter prediction also helps on unique cards | Derived from 4 | 2048 sample, scd=0, semantic order, ssimulacra2 on both arms | Refuted: ties tune=still on bytes but ssim2 p50 falls from 62.7 to 46.8; the probe now vetoes it |
| 6 | Tune IQ and inter prediction stack | Assistant | tune=3 with GOP 9999 | Refuted: SVT-AV1 accepts IQ only in all-intra or low-delay, and low-delay costs +21% |
| 7 | A lossless path better than JPEG exists, "even if we have to invent it" | User | Ten-generation battery: JXL e7/e9, JXL plus zstd, jpegtran, jpegoptim, WebP, FFV1 in two orders, x264 qp0 | Refuted: the ceiling is jxl-transcode at 1.12x; ordered lossless video loses 3.3 to 3.8x |
| 8 | tar plus zstd compresses JPEG | Ruler | tar --zstd at level 19 over the 2048 files | Refuted: 1.00x |
| 9 | art_crop is derivable from normal, saving 3 GB of raw cache | Assistant | An agent read the app code and schemas | Refuted: no crop coordinates are stored, and the app uses art_crop in its deck tiles |
| 10 | Equal bytes at equal CRF mean equal quality | Implicit in the first GOP probe | ssimulacra2 on both arms plus mad alignment against the source | Refuted: SVT quantizes P-frames coarser; the probe became quality-aware with a 2-point tolerance |
| 11 | CLIP cosine drift is a good proxy of search utility | Premise of the dual gate | Retrieval CRF 40 to 60, hit@k on 100 queries | Refuted: drift falls fast (0.932 at crf40, 0.829 at crf60) while txt@1 stays flat up to crf50 |
| 12 | AVIF from libaom beats SVT-AV1 all-intra | I-frame battery | 19 encoders calibrated to ssimulacra2 mean 61.96 | Confirmed: -12.4% on the sample, -13.0% on the full .urna |
| 13 | Models that read the printed text win text-to-image search | Model bench | T3 with "artwork of the card {name}", 60 queries on the 1500 sample | Confirmed: wemm-2b@256 hits 0.933 at rank 1 against CLIP's 0.233 |

Hypothesis 4 is confirmed on bytes and the quality cost of that confirmation is what refutes hypothesis 5 on unique cards; the two are the same lever seen from two corpora. Hypothesis 11 is the one that changed the product contract: the drift floor stays as a stability gate, and a retrieval-only profile needs a utility floor that does not exist yet.

Corrections the audit made to numbers quoted earlier in this changelog. Bytes are exact; MB is bytes / 1e6 and GB is bytes / 1e9, decimal. The 2026-09-03 report read `du`, which counts allocated blocks in binary units, and labelled MiB as GB.

- The retrieval crf50 file is 532,671,548 bytes = 0.533 GB, 7.46x on the `.urna` and 8.06x on the media against the 3,975,063,106-byte JPEG source, not "508 MB / 7.8x" (508 was MiB; 7.8x was the source divided by 508e6).
- The AVIF q48 manifest recorded `media.source_bytes` 21,450,566,470 and `compression_ratio` 18.61 because the AVIF backend summed its letterboxed PNG intermediates instead of the JPEG sources, the bug fixed in #132. The true source is the same 3,975,063,106 bytes, so the candidate (1,195,973,116 bytes) is 3.32x on the `.urna` and 3.45x on the media. Against the neardup release it is -13.0% on the `.urna`, which is the -12.4% the I-frame battery predicted at matched quality: the prediction held, it did not come out larger at full scale.
- The 2048-card sample is evenly spaced, `rows[int(i * 38627 / 2048)]` over the rows sorted by (img_id, oracle_id), deterministic and seed independent; "seed 42" was a provenance error, the forge `--seed` flag has no effect on it. The only seeded draws are the 96 quality frames and the 100 utility queries (`numpy.random.default_rng(7)`).

The open follow-ups are GitHub issues in the benchmark repository: a hit@k floor for a retrieval-only gate, wemm-2b and Jina measured over the crf50 file, AVIF as the `stills` backend, and a recalibration of the gate floors.

[0.5.0]: https://github.com/hoffresearch/urna/releases/tag/v0.5.0

## [0.3.0] - 2026-06-10

Additive release within frozen format v1. Extends v0.2.0 with the int4 sub-int8 lever and the published preset ladder, the g1 graph pillar, Matryoshka prefix truncation, and the forge-core ingestion workspace. Existing v0.2 files load unchanged in v0.3 readers.

### Note (2026-06-07): recall ruler provenance


### Added

int4 block-64 embeddings, the first real sub-int8 size lever (additive within v1, no format-version bump):

- `encoding=7` int4 embeddings. Layout: 8-byte prefix (`payload_version=1`, `scale_kind=1`), then per-64-dim-group f16 absmax scales (row-major), then packed 4-bit signed codes (`[-7, 7]`, two nibbles per byte, low nibble first). Requires `dtype="int4"` and `embedding_dim` divisible by 64. Each 64-dim block carries its own scale so one block's outlier cannot crush another. Validated like int8: the 4-bit codes cannot encode NaN/Inf, only the f16 group scales are range-checked.
- Fused dequant+dot kernel in the runtime SIMD module (`dot_f32_i4_blocked`): AVX2 and NEON vectorize the nibble unpack 16 packed bytes at a time, then run the identical per-group scalar reduction, so all three backends agree bit-for-bit (a lane-parallel float reduction would diverge in the last ulp). The embeddings section is scored straight off mmap; like int8 it is never zstd/dedup/shuffled.
- The mandatory exact rerank reads the stored int4 slab (no separate full-precision source, exactly like int8), so the returned `score` is real cosine AT THE INT4 STORED PRECISION. Disclosed via `manifest.dtype` and the `dtype=int4` / `encoding=int4` lines in `urna stats`, never reported as a bare-slab ratio.
- `nano` preset: zstd text, int4 embeddings, HNSW, no BM25. Sits below `tiny`. On the project's PT-BR corpus (n=30,725, dim=384, NEON, 100 queries, k=10): embeddings section 11.92 MB (int8) -> 6.27 MB (int4), ~1.9x over int8 and ~7.5x over float32; file size_ratio 0.209; recall@10 0.913 vs the float32 exact baseline.
- `dtype` extended to `"float32" | "float16" | "int8" | "int4"`. Python `build(..., preset="nano")` or `dtype="int4"` rejects `embedding_dim` not divisible by 64 with a typed error.
- `compare_measure.py` gains conditional `nano` regression gates (`size_ratio <= 0.25`, `recall_at_k >= 0.85`), active only when the run includes the nano preset.

Scope note: rabitq (encoding 8), dedup-before-zstd (0x0B), and chunk_scalars (0x0D) are the other legs of the same plan task and are not part of this change.

Published preset ladder + honest net-of-fp reconciliation (measurement and docs only, no format/runtime change):

- `measure_presets.py` names two explicit sub-int8 ladder rungs: `nano` (int4 full-dim) and `micro` (the Matryoshka lever, an alias for `mrl256-int8`: `mrl_dim=256` + int8). The default `--variants` now emit the full ladder `compressed,tiny,micro,nano,hybrid` plus the MRL curve `mrl256/192/128-int8`, `mrl96-int8`, `mrl256/192/128-int4`.
- Honest reconciliation: the intpack chunk_ids/spans repack and the bitpacked HNSW/BM25 payloads shrank the indexed presets below the v0.2 published figures. The committed numbers now match the build: `tiny` 0.283 -> 0.256, `compressed` 0.350 -> 0.339, `hybrid` 0.668 -> 0.609. recall@10 unchanged (`tiny` 0.992, `compressed`/`hybrid` 1.000).
- Net-of-fp framing: the 0x09 `embeddings_fp` writer is not wired, so every shipped sub-int8 preset (`nano`/int4, `micro`/mrl-int8, the mrl-int4 curve) is STORED-PRECISION: net-of-fp ratio == stored ratio, disclosed as real cosine at the stored int4/int8 precision. Each published sub-int8 row carries `dtype`, `stored_precision`, `has_fp_source=false`, `net_of_fp_ratio`, and (for Matryoshka rows) `mrl_dim`/`full_dim`, so the disclosure is machine-checkable. rabitq 1-bit (which would need a counted f16 fp source) stays out of scope.
- `compare_measure.py` gains a conditional `micro` gate (`size_ratio <= 0.25`, `recall_at_k >= 0.78`), mirroring the existing `nano` and `mrl256-int8` gates; it fires only when the run includes `micro`.
- Published numbers (100 queries, k=10, vs the float32 exact baseline): `compressed` 0.339 / 1.000, `tiny` 0.256 / 0.992, `micro` 0.223 / 0.810, `nano` 0.209 / 0.913, `hybrid` 0.609 / 1.000.

g1 graph pillar: chunk-to-chunk graph adjacency (section 0x0C) + `search-graph` (additive within v1, no format-version bump):

- New optional section `0x0C graph_adjacency`: a chunk-to-chunk CSR with typed edges (`NEXT_CHUNK=0`, `SEMANTIC=1`, `CITATION=2`). The payload is a self-describing raw section reusing intpack internally: `GRAPH_ADJACENCY_PAYLOAD_VERSION=1`, n_nodes, intpack CSR offsets, per-(src,edge_type)-run delta-gapped neighbor ids, and a FalkorDB-iso edge-type column (a single scalar when uniform). Canonical ascending (src, edge_type, dst) sort makes two builds of the same edge set byte-identical.
- Excluded from `content_hash` (not in `CANONICAL_SECTIONS`), so adding a graph never invalidates a `urna://` citation. Gated by the additive `capabilities_ext.graph_present` flag and opened in `mmap_file` behind it.
- Runtime `graph::CsrIndex` parses the section off mmap into flat offsets plus neighbors (one node's neighbors are a contiguous zero-alloc slice); `graph::Traversal::bounded_bfs` walks it with a generational visited buffer (no per-query HashSet), bounded by hops and max_frontier.
- 9th engine subcommand `urna search-graph <file> <qvec> -k K --hops N --ef N`: seeds the exact-cosine top-ef, BFS-expands the union, and feeds it to the SAME `score_subset` exact rerank (`index_type=graph`, `recall=NaN`; the candidate-generator contract is asserted in `rerank_contract.rs`). Falls back to exact when no 0x0C section is present.
- Build side: `urna.build(with_graph=True, graph_top_m=...)` emits NEXT_CHUNK (sequential ordinals, both directions) plus top-m SEMANTIC edges from the already-built HNSW level-0 graph. `drop_overlap` implies `with_graph`; read-side neighbor context via `python/graph_context.py`; recall gate in `python/tools/graph_recall_gate.py`.

Matryoshka prefix truncation (`mrl_dim`), the build-time dimension lever (additive within v1):

- `urna.build(mrl_dim=K)` slices each l2-normalized row to its first K components and re-l2-normalizes the prefix BEFORE quantization and HNSW, so int8/int4 calibrate on the shorter renormalized row. Orthogonal to and multiplicative with the dtype levers.
- Additive optional manifest pair `mrl_dim`/`full_dim` (both omitted when unset, so existing files stay byte-identical); the stored `embedding_dim` becomes K, the source dim is recorded as `full_dim`, both shown in `urna stats`.
- NO runtime kernel change: the reader strides by `header.embedding_dim`. int4 needs the effective dim divisible by 64, so the int4 ladder is valid only at `mrl_dim` in {256, 192, 128}.
- `content_hash` is over the truncated embeddings, so a citation is tied to its `mrl_dim`, never claimed stable across dims. Truncation is a pure deterministic slice, so builds stay byte-identical; guarded by `tests/mrl_truncate.rs`.
- On the shipped non-MRL MiniLM baseline truncation costs real recall@10, published as a curve (same self-perturbation ruler caveat as the note above); `mrl256-int8` is the named `micro` preset.

forge-core (FORGE-0a): the ingestion layer's frozen .fci schema, in a separate workspace:

- New cargo workspace `forge-core/` at the repo root, deliberately OUTSIDE `crates/`, so its dependency tree never enters the sovereign crates; not built by the sovereign release gate. Build, test, and lint it via `--manifest-path forge-core/Cargo.toml`.
- FORGE-0a ships the frozen .fci canonical-intermediate schema only: `FciBundle`, `ChunkRecord` (mirroring `builder.ChunkSpec` 1:1 so spans round-trip through `urna cite`), `EmbeddingRequest`/`SpaceTag`/`PayloadRef`, `Entity`/`MentionSpan`/`Edge`, and `BlobRef`.
- `FCI_SCHEMA_VERSION` is versioned independently of `URNA_FORMAT_VERSION`. Serialization is deterministic canonical compact JSON (declaration order, verbatim strings), covered by roundtrip and determinism tests.

The agent-native flagship (p1#08), over the existing engine:

- CLI `ask` and `retrieve`, and Python `UrnaFile.retrieve`: the query is embedded offline with the potion table, gated by `model_hash`, routed by the manifest's capabilities, and answered with tier-1 cited spans whose score is the exact rerank value.
- The additive `SearchExplain` reports `rerank_source`: real cosine, or real cosine at the stored precision.

### Test surface

- 288 Rust tests in the sovereign workspace (`cargo test --release --workspace`, 35 suites; was 134 in v0.2.0), plus 6 forge-core tests on its own manifest (`cargo test --manifest-path forge-core/Cargo.toml`).
- New groups since v0.2.0: txt_streams roundtrip plus negatives, zstd_dict roundtrip plus negatives, fsst roundtrip plus negatives, dedup roundtrip plus order-invariant, content_hash_dict_fsst_dedup, graph_adjacency roundtrip plus negatives, int4 roundtrip plus negatives, mrl_truncate, manifest_additivity, reserved_ids, the expanded rerank_contract (graph path, SearchExplain, stored-precision disclosure), and forge-core serialize.
- Python: the 4 test scripts run by `release_check.sh` (`test_e2e.py` incl the flagship retrieve guard, `test_builder.py`, `test_search_text_model_hash.py`, `test_image_corpus.py` at 15 cases) plus the self-test scripts under `python/forge/` (potion, lexical floor, retrieve), which are not.

### Compatibility

No format break. The new optional sections (0x0A dictionary, 0x0B dedup_map, 0x0C graph_adjacency), wire encodings (4 intpack, 5 zstd_dict, 7 int4, 9 fsst, 10 txt_streams), and additive manifest fields (mrl_dim/full_dim, capabilities_ext) all live within v1. v0.2 readers skip the unknown optional sections and reject the new encodings with a typed error, never silently. Files built without the new features stay byte-identical.

[0.3.0]: https://github.com/hoffresearch/urna/releases/tag/v0.3.0

## [0.2.0] - 2026-04-28

Production-ready release. Extends v1 with new section encodings, optional ANN and lexical sections, runtime SIMD dispatch, and offline model verification. Existing v0.1 files load unchanged in v0.2 readers.

### Added

Section encodings within v1 (no format-version bump):

- `encoding=1` zstd for text-heavy sections (`chunks_canonical`, `chunks_original_spans`, `provenance`, `search_contract`, `bm25_index`). Reader decodes transparently. Embeddings are never zstd-compressed.
- `encoding=2` float16 embeddings. Writer converts f32 to f16; runtime decodes lane-by-lane and accumulates in f32.
- `encoding=3` int8 embeddings. Per-vector f32 scale plus n*dim i8 quantized values. Always paired with rerank against an exact path or HNSW.

Optional sections (skipped by older readers, not part of `content_hash`):

- `0x07 hnsw_index`. Pure-Rust HNSW (Malkov-Yashunin Algorithm 4 heuristic). Build is deterministic given a seed. Search returns candidates; runtime always reranks with the exact dot product so the final score is real cosine.
- `0x08 bm25_index`. Inverted index over tokenized chunk text. Used by hybrid search via reciprocal-rank fusion (RRF, k=60) with the vector path, then exact rerank on the union.

CLI:

- `urna search-ann <file> <qvec> -k K --ef N`: force the HNSW path with explicit `ef_search`.
- `urna search-text <file> "query" -k K [--model-path PATH] [--skip-model-hash-check]`: shells out to `python/embed_query.py`, validates the embedder's `embedding_model`, `embedding_dim`, and `model_hash` against the manifest, then routes to the declared `index_type` (exact, HNSW, hybrid). A model mismatch fails with a typed error, never silently.
- `urna benchmark --madvise-cold`: extra benchmark pass calling `posix_madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)` between queries. Upper bound on cold-cache latency, not absolute cold (see `MmapUrnaFile::madvise_cold` for caveats).
- `urna inspect --json`: structured output mirroring `MmapUrnaFile::inspect_json` for programmatic consumers.

Build presets:

| Preset       | Text encoding | Embeddings | ANN | BM25 | size_ratio | recall@10 |
|--------------|---------------|------------|-----|------|-----------:|----------:|
| `exact`      | raw           | float32    | no  | no   |      1.000 |    1.0000 |
| `compressed` | zstd          | float16    | no  | no   |      0.350 |    1.0000 |
| `tiny`       | zstd          | int8       | yes | no   |      0.283 |    0.9920 |
| `hybrid`     | zstd          | float32    | yes | yes  |      0.668 |    1.0000 |

Measured on the project's PT-BR fake-news corpus (n=30,725, dim=384, NEON, 100 queries, k=10).

SIMD dispatch:

- Per-dtype dot-product backends: AVX2 on x86_64, NEON on aarch64, scalar fallback.
- Detection at runtime via `is_x86_feature_detected!` / `is_aarch64_feature_detected!`.
- `URNA_FORCE_SCALAR=1` forces the scalar fallback for A/B benchmarks.
- Accumulators always in f32 regardless of dtype.

Model fingerprint:

- `python/model_fingerprint.py`: reproducible model fingerprint composed of `{model_id, files_hash, tokenizer_hash, pooling_config_hash, embedding_dim, normalize_embeddings}`. JCS-canonical JSON, hashed to produce the manifest's `model_hash`.
- Builder refuses to write the legacy zero-placeholder (`sha256:0...0`).
- `python/embed_query.py` emits structured JSON: `{model_hash, fingerprint, embedding_model, embedding_dim, vector}`.
- CLI accepts `--model-path /path/to/snapshot` for fully-offline operation.

Manifest:

- `dtype` extended to `"float32" | "float16" | "int8"`.
- `index_type` extended to `"exact" | "hnsw" | "hybrid"`.
- `rerank_policy` extended to `"none" | "exact"`.
- `capabilities.supports_ann` and `capabilities.supports_bm25` reflect the optional sections actually present.

Tooling:

- `python/tools/measure_presets.py`: builds all 4 presets from a baseline `.urna`, measures size / recall / score drift / p50/p95/p99 latency. Emits Markdown table or `--json` for regression gates.
- `python/tools/compare_measure.py`: validates two `--json` dumps against 6 production gates (size ratios, recall floors, p95 headroom). Non-zero exit on any failure.
- `scripts/release_check.sh`: end-to-end CI gate (build, test, Clippy, fmt, line-count guard, Python tests, ruff, measure, compare). Single source of truth for "PR-ready".

Infrastructure:

- HNSW recall fix: replaced naive `top-m` neighbor selection with `select_neighbors_heuristic` (Malkov-Yashunin Algorithm 4). Bumped `DEFAULT_EF_CONSTRUCTION` to 400 to hit recall@10 >= 0.95 at typical corpus sizes.
- File hygiene cap: every Rust source file in `crates/**/src/**` and every first-party Python module is at most 300 lines. Test files exempt.

### Test surface

- 134 Rust tests (was 70 in v0.1):
  - 47 `urna-format` unit + roundtrip tests
  - New: `dual_integrity.rs` (3 cases: encoding-invariant content_hash, mismatched section/file hashes)
  - New: `negative_zstd.rs` (3 cases: bad encoding values, embedding zstd refusal, encoding-mismatch)
  - New: `negative_fp16.rs` (4 cases: NaN, Inf, odd dim SIMD parity)
  - New: `negative_int8.rs` (4 cases: bad payload version, bad scale_kind, NaN scale, truncation)
  - New: `v01_compat.rs` (1 case: golden v0.1 fixture loads in v0.2 reader byte-identical)
  - New: `hnsw_recall.rs` (3 cases: recall@10 >= 0.95, recall@1 >= 0.90, realistic-size sanity)
  - New: `fp16_topk_recall_vs_f32.rs` (1 case: recall@10 >= 0.98 vs f32, drift <= 1e-4)
- Python: 3 test scripts (`test_e2e.py`, `test_builder.py`, `test_search_text_model_hash.py` with 5 cases).
- `cargo fmt --all --check` clean. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean. `ruff check . && ruff format --check .` clean.

### Compatibility

v0.1 files (raw + float32 + 6 required sections) load unchanged in v0.2 readers. The byte-frozen golden fixture at `crates/urna-format/tests/fixtures/golden_v1_minimal.urna` is verified every CI run.

v0.2 files that use only `encoding=raw` and `dtype=float32` still load in v0.1 readers, with one caveat: optional sections 0x07 and 0x08 are unknown to v0.1 readers and get skipped (file still loads). v0.1 readers reject `encoding ∈ {1,2,3}` and `dtype ∈ {"float16", "int8"}` with `UnsupportedSectionEncoding` or `UnsupportedDType`, never silently.

[0.2.0]: https://github.com/hoffresearch/urna/releases/tag/v0.2.0

## [0.1.0] - 2026-04-27

First public release. The on-disk format, hash semantics, citation URI, manifest contract, and CLI surface listed below are frozen for v1: any change must bump `URNA_FORMAT_VERSION` (binary container) or `URNA_SCHEMA_VERSION` (manifest fields).

### Frozen binary container

- File magic: `NEST` (`0x4E 0x45 0x53 0x54`); renamed to `URNA` in the unreleased section above.
- `URNA_VERSION_MAJOR = 1`, `URNA_VERSION_MINOR = 0`, `URNA_FORMAT_VERSION = 1`, `URNA_SCHEMA_VERSION = 1`.
- Header: 128 bytes, `repr(C)`, compile-time asserted. Fields (LE, unsigned): `magic`, `version_major/minor`, `flags`, `embedding_dim`, `n_chunks`, `n_embeddings`, `file_size`, `section_table_offset`, `section_table_count`, `manifest_offset`, `manifest_size`, `header_checksum[8]`, `reserved[48]`.
- Section table entry: 32 bytes, `repr(C)`, compile-time asserted. Fields: `section_id(u32)`, `encoding(u32)`, `offset(u64)`, `size(u64)`, `checksum[8]`.
- Footer: 40 bytes (`u64 footer_size = 40`, `[u8; 32] file_hash`).
- Section payload alignment: every section's `offset` is a multiple of `SECTION_ALIGNMENT = 64`. Padding between sections is zero and excluded from each section's checksum (but covered by the footer hash).
- Endianness: little-endian, unsigned unless explicitly noted.

### Required sections (canonical, alphabetical for `content_hash`)

| ID     | Name                       |
| ------ | -------------------------- |
| `0x01` | `chunk_ids`                |
| `0x02` | `chunks_canonical`         |
| `0x03` | `chunks_original_spans`    |
| `0x04` | `embeddings`               |
| `0x05` | `provenance`               |
| `0x06` | `search_contract`          |

Encoding: only `SECTION_ENCODING_RAW = 0` is accepted in v0.1. Values `1 = zstd`, `2 = float16`, `3 = int8` are reserved (and shipped in v0.2).

### Hashing

- Primary hash: SHA-256 throughout. No BLAKE3.
- `header_checksum`: first 8 bytes of `SHA-256(header[0..72] ++ header[80..128])`. Header with its own checksum slot zeroed.
- Section `checksum`: first 8 bytes of `SHA-256(payload)`. Padding is not hashed.
- `file_hash` (footer): full 32-byte `SHA-256(file[0..file_size-40))`, including padding.
- `content_hash`: 32-byte `SHA-256` over the canonical sections in alphabetical-by-name order, each domain-separated by length-prefixed name and length-prefixed payload. Stable across rebuilds of the same content.
- `chunk_id`: domain-separated `SHA-256` with literal preimage prefix `"urna:chunk_id:v1\n"`. Format `sha256:<64 hex chars>`.
- `model_hash`: caller-supplied; format `sha256:<64 hex chars>` enforced at write time. v0.1 accepted any value matching the regex; v0.2 enforces a real fingerprint.

### Manifest contract

JCS-style canonical JSON (declaration-ordered known fields, BTreeMap order for `extra`, no whitespace). Required values for v0.1:

- `dtype = "float32"`, `metric = "ip"`, `score_type = "cosine"`, `normalize = "l2"`, `index_type = "exact"`, `rerank_policy = "none"`.
- `capabilities.supports_exact = true`, `capabilities.supports_reproducible_build = true`.
- `model_hash` matches `sha256:<64 hex>` regex.

### Reproducibility

- `UrnaFileBuilder::reproducible(true)` overrides `manifest.created` to `"1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"` (`REPRODUCIBLE_CREATED`).
- Two builds with identical inputs produce byte-identical files. Verified on the legacy converter (`data/truw_ptbr.urna` to 73.73 MB v0.1 binary); both builds shasum to `b9f6e0ea16176706f08767559927737ce91070147ec6cb54e26710bff3d2566d`.

### Version skew policy

- Reader rejects `format_version` or `schema_version` greater than its own constants (`UrnaError::UnsupportedFormatVersion` / `UnsupportedSchemaVersion`).
- Reader accepts equal or smaller versions.
- Header version: `version_major != 1` rejected; `version_minor` may drift downward.

### CLI v0.1 surface

Frozen subcommands in `urna-cli`:

| Command           | Behavior                                                           |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `urna inspect`    | Header, section table, manifest, hashes                            |
| `urna validate`   | Full integrity check (header / sections / footer / manifest)        |
| `urna stats`      | Size, chunk count, dim, model, hashes, per-section sizes            |
| `urna search`     | Exact top-k search; query is a JSON array of f32                    |
| `urna benchmark`  | Latency stats over N random queries                                |
| `urna cite`       | Resolve `urna://content_hash/chunk_id` to `(text, span, hashes)`    |

The v0.1 CLI does not ship an embedding model. Text to vector is the caller's responsibility (see `python/builder.py` and `python/convert_legacy.py` for examples using sentence-transformers). v0.2 added `urna search-text` to fill this gap.

### Citation URI

`urna://<content_hash>/<chunk_id>` where both halves are full `sha256:<hex>` strings. `urna cite` rejects citations whose `content_hash` does not match the file's `content_hash`.

### Search contract

`SearchHit` exposes: `chunk_id`, `score` (real f32 cosine in `[-1, 1]`), `score_type = "cosine"`, `source_uri`, `offset_start`, `offset_end`, `embedding_model`, `index_type = "exact"`, `reranked = false`, `file_hash`, `content_hash`, `citation_id`. Top-k uses a stable sort by score descending, breaking ties by index ascending. `recall = 1.0` always; `truncated = (k < n_embeddings)`.

### Test surface

- 70 Rust tests (`cargo test --workspace`):
  - 34 `urna-format` unit tests (layout, manifest, sections, chunk, writer)
  - 5 `urna-format` golden-fixture tests (1366-byte minimal `.urna`)
  - 19 `urna-format` roundtrip / negative tests (truncation, magic, encoding, alignment, version skew, dim mismatch, NaN/Inf)
  - 8 `urna-runtime` flat-search tests
  - 4 `urna-cli` integration tests (`validate`, `search`, `inspect`, `cite`)
- 4 Python tests (`tests/test_e2e.py`, PyO3-only) and 3 builder tests (`tests/test_builder.py`).
- `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; `cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` clean.

### Python bindings

`python/_urna.so` (PyO3, abi3-py312). Wrapper `python/urna.py` exposes:

- `urna.open(path) -> UrnaFile`
- `UrnaFile.search(qvec, k) -> [SearchHit]`
- `UrnaFile.inspect()` / `UrnaFile.validate()`
- `UrnaFile.embedding_dim` / `n_embeddings` / `file_hash` / `content_hash`
- `urna.build(...)` (writer glue)
- `urna.chunk_id(...)` (deterministic id derivation)

Single Python entry point: no subprocess CLI fallback inside `python/`.

### Reference artefacts

- Golden fixture: `crates/urna-format/tests/fixtures/golden_v1_minimal.urna` (1366 bytes; regenerate with `cargo run -p urna-format --example regen_golden`).
- Legacy SQLite-based dataset: `data/truw_ptbr.urna` (28 MB) to `data/truw_ptbr.v1.urna` (73.73 MB, 19,769 chunks dim 384) via `python/convert_legacy.py`.
- Architecture references: `docs/arc/arc.yaml` and `docs/arc/arc.mmd`.

[0.1.0]: https://github.com/hoffresearch/urna/releases/tag/v0.1.0
